[Event "Chapter 1: The 150 Attack (Solid Lines) / Study 1: White Castles Short (Part 1) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/f4307662-b346-4aa6-8ee1-fd9ac4fd7d53/studies/f2aec3d4-cfcf-4ba4-8350-d1f63607e143/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of the first chapter! This study will cover some of the mainline with 4. Be3, as well as 4.Bf4 and 4.Bg5 for White. White moves their center pawn to e4, which is the most popular move at all levels. The Modern Defense starts with 1... g6. Black's idea is to put the Bishop on g7, and depending on how White develops, Black will combat in the center.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Our bishop will be nicely placed on g7 to attack the center from afar. Black does not have an immediate threat, so White has some flexibility in this position.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {This is the main line by White, and one of the more aggressive schemes. White's idea may be to develop the dark-squared bishop, move the queen to d2, castle long, and launch a kingside attack. For us, ...d6 is played so that our knight can hop to d7, and prepare an ...e5 or ...c5 break in the center at some point.} 3...d6 4.Be3 {4.Be3 is played more than 30% of the time, as White protects d4 and the bishop is harder to attack. The main idea behind ...a6 is to prepare ...b5, which will gain space on the queenside and give the light-squared bishop an outlet on b7.} 4...a6 5.a4 {White's idea is to stop b5. However, White is indicating that they are not intending to castle queenside after a4. Black develops a knight, targeting the center.} 5...Nf6 6.h3 {This follows the game Goryachinka-Sadhwani 2020. 6.h3 is also designed to stop ...Ng4. Nothing wrong with castling!} 6...O-O 7.g4 {White's intentions are clear. We can continue developing towards the center. ...Nc6 eyes d4, and plans ...e5.} 7...Nc6 8.g5 {White forces us to move our knight. d7 is the only square that makes sense.} 8...Nd7 9.h4 {White continues the assault.} 9...e5 {When we're attacked on a flank, we counter in the center! If White pushes to d5, it's crucial to stay active and jump to d4 instead of d7. That way, if White pushes h5, we have options open to play ...f5 or ...f6.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 150 Attack (Solid Lines) / Study 1: White Castles Short (Part 1) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/f4307662-b346-4aa6-8ee1-fd9ac4fd7d53/studies/f2aec3d4-cfcf-4ba4-8350-d1f63607e143/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of the first chapter! This study will cover some of the mainline with 4. Be3, as well as 4.Bf4 and 4.Bg5 for White. White moves their center pawn to e4, which is the most popular move at all levels. The Modern Defense starts with 1... g6. Black's idea is to put the Bishop on g7, and depending on how White develops, Black will combat in the center.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Our bishop will be nicely placed on g7 to attack the center from afar. Black does not have an immediate threat, so White has some flexibility in this position.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {This is the main line by White, and one of the more aggressive schemes. White's idea may be to develop the dark-squared bishop, move the queen to d2, castle long, and launch a kingside attack. For us, ...d6 is played so that our knight can hop to d7, and prepare an ...e5 or ...c5 break in the center at some point.} 3...d6 4.Be3 {4.Be3 is played more than 30% of the time, as White protects d4 and the bishop is harder to attack. The main idea behind ...a6 is to prepare ...b5, which will gain space on the queenside and give the light-squared bishop an outlet on b7.} 4...a6 5.a4 {White's idea is to stop b5. However, White is indicating that they are not intending to castle queenside after a4. Black develops a knight, targeting the center.} 5...Nf6 6.f3 {This is a common move to stop ...Ng4 while also preparing a g4-h4 assault as well. We'll just castle. Nothing out of the ordinary.} 6...O-O 7.Qd2 {White prepares the possibilities of castling long and/or going Bh6. We develop towards the center.} 7...Nc6 8.Nge2 {Nge2 protects d4. Once we have activated most of our minor piece and White's central presence becomes annoying... We can finally attack the center!} 8...e5 9.d5 {White may prompt the knight to retreat.} 9...Ne7 {Black can spice up counterplay in several ways. These include ...c6 to fight for the center, or maneuvering the f6-knight to d7 and striking with ...f5. Either way, we have to fight against White's pawn structure somehow.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 150 Attack (Solid Lines) / Study 1: White Castles Short (Part 1) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/f4307662-b346-4aa6-8ee1-fd9ac4fd7d53/studies/f2aec3d4-cfcf-4ba4-8350-d1f63607e143/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of the first chapter! This study will cover some of the mainline with 4. Be3, as well as 4.Bf4 and 4.Bg5 for White. White moves their center pawn to e4, which is the most popular move at all levels. The Modern Defense starts with 1... g6. Black's idea is to put the Bishop on g7, and depending on how White develops, Black will combat in the center.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Our bishop will be nicely placed on g7 to attack the center from afar. Black does not have an immediate threat, so White has some flexibility in this position.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {This is the main line by White, and one of the more aggressive schemes. White's idea may be to develop the dark-squared bishop, move the queen to d2, castle long, and launch a kingside attack. For us, ...d6 is played so that our knight can hop to d7, and prepare an ...e5 or ...c5 break in the center at some point.} 3...d6 4.Bf4 {This is a move you might see White play, but it's not accurate, as the bishop on f4 can be a target for ...e5 in the future. It's played only 3% of the time. ...Nc6 attacks d4, and there are a few logical ways for White to defend.} 4...Nc6 5.Bb5 {White may play Bb5 straight away to pin the knight on c6, contributing to the attack of the pawn on d4. We simply develop our knight and prepare to castle.} 5...Nf6 6.d5 {This is something we need to think about, as d5 attacks the pinned knight. But there's a good way to counter this. And that's attacking the bishop, so that dxc6 is less scary of a threat.} 6...a6 7.Ba4 {White may drop the bishop to keep the pin alive. Breaking the pin.} 7...b5 8.Nxb5 {White may find this sacrifice if they're smart. Capturing is the only logical move.} 8...axb5 9.Bxb5 {White temporarily sacrifices a piece, but renews the pin/threat on the knight. Castling forces White to take the knight this move, as now the pin is broken.} 9...O-O 10.Bxc6 {Taking with the bishop makes the most sense, as White threatens the rook.} 10...Rb8 {Even though Black is down two pawns, there's plenty of compensation. It's in the form of multiple hanging pawns, open lines, and a lead in development. Black should be doing just fine.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 150 Attack (Solid Lines) / Study 1: White Castles Short (Part 1) / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/f4307662-b346-4aa6-8ee1-fd9ac4fd7d53/studies/f2aec3d4-cfcf-4ba4-8350-d1f63607e143/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of the first chapter! This study will cover some of the mainline with 4. Be3, as well as 4.Bf4 and 4.Bg5 for White. White moves their center pawn to e4, which is the most popular move at all levels. The Modern Defense starts with 1... g6. Black's idea is to put the Bishop on g7, and depending on how White develops, Black will combat in the center.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Our bishop will be nicely placed on g7 to attack the center from afar. Black does not have an immediate threat, so White has some flexibility in this position.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {This is the main line by White, and one of the more aggressive schemes. White's idea may be to develop the dark-squared bishop, move the queen to d2, castle long, and launch a kingside attack. For us, ...d6 is played so that our knight can hop to d7, and prepare an ...e5 or ...c5 break in the center at some point.} 3...d6 4.Bg5 {This is a move we might also see, possibly with the idea to pin the e-pawn, which could be a nuisance. It's played 5% of the time. We begin our plan to expand on the queenside, especially in case White castles over there.} 4...a6 5.Qd2 {White keeps castling options flexible. We continue expanding on the queenside, as is our plan.} 5...b5 6.a4 {White may try to break up our structure. However, a4 generally indicates that White won't castle queenside. Our most natural response is to continue expanding, while attacking the knight.} 6...b4 7.Nd1 {Most reasonable retreat for White. We don't need to worry about defending the b4-pawn, as White taking it will cost them some tempi in the future. We can just continue developing instead.} 7...Nd7 8.Qxb4 {White doesn't have to take the pawn, but it's probably the critical line. Threatening a pawn and developing a piece.} 8...Ngf6 9.f3 {Defends e4 solidly with a pawn. But ...c5 threatens the queen and strikes in the center.} 9...c5 10.dxc5 {Taking is natural as moving the queen allows us to take on d4.} 10...Nxc5 {Black is up in development, and has sufficient compensation for the pawn. We'll finish queenside development and take the b-file, after which we'll castle short.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 150 Attack (Solid Lines) / Study 1: White Castles Short (Part 1) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/f4307662-b346-4aa6-8ee1-fd9ac4fd7d53/studies/f2aec3d4-cfcf-4ba4-8350-d1f63607e143/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of the first chapter! This study will cover some of the mainline with 4. Be3, as well as 4.Bf4 and 4.Bg5 for White. White moves their center pawn to e4, which is the most popular move at all levels. The Modern Defense starts with 1... g6. Black's idea is to put the Bishop on g7, and depending on how White develops, Black will combat in the center.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Our bishop will be nicely placed on g7 to attack the center from afar. Black does not have an immediate threat, so White has some flexibility in this position.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {This is the main line by White, and one of the more aggressive schemes. White's idea may be to develop the dark-squared bishop, move the queen to d2, castle long, and launch a kingside attack. For us, ...d6 is played so that our knight can hop to d7, and prepare an ...e5 or ...c5 break in the center at some point.} 3...d6 4.Bf4 {This is a move you might see White play, but it's not accurate, as the bishop on f4 can be a target for ...e5 in the future. It's played only 3% of the time. ...Nc6 attacks d4, and there are a few logical ways for White to defend.} 4...Nc6 5.d5 {Attacking the knight with the pawn is one logical way to do it. We can counterattack the bishop while placing a pawn in the center. If White takes the knight or takes en passant, we will be able to open our bishop.} 5...e5 6.Be3 {Retreating the bishop is also very logical.} 6...Nce7 {And we can begin to launch our kingside attack. The move ...f5 is important to fight in the center and give our pieces some space. After that, we'll develop behind the pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 150 Attack (Solid Lines) / Study 1: White Castles Short (Part 1) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/f4307662-b346-4aa6-8ee1-fd9ac4fd7d53/studies/f2aec3d4-cfcf-4ba4-8350-d1f63607e143/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of the first chapter! This study will cover some of the mainline with 4. Be3, as well as 4.Bf4 and 4.Bg5 for White. White moves their center pawn to e4, which is the most popular move at all levels. The Modern Defense starts with 1... g6. Black's idea is to put the Bishop on g7, and depending on how White develops, Black will combat in the center.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Our bishop will be nicely placed on g7 to attack the center from afar. Black does not have an immediate threat, so White has some flexibility in this position.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {This is the main line by White, and one of the more aggressive schemes. White's idea may be to develop the dark-squared bishop, move the queen to d2, castle long, and launch a kingside attack. For us, ...d6 is played so that our knight can hop to d7, and prepare an ...e5 or ...c5 break in the center at some point.} 3...d6 4.Bf4 {This is a move you might see White play, but it's not accurate, as the bishop on f4 can be a target for ...e5 in the future. It's played only 3% of the time. ...Nc6 attacks d4, and there are a few logical ways for White to defend.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {Nf3 is a logical attempt to defend the pawn on d4. However, White has to be careful after the following move. ...Bg4 pins the knight which is protecting d4, and capturing is an immediate threat.} 5...Bg4 6.Bb5 {White may try to defend d4 by pinning the knight on c6. This still exploits d4, as the natural Qxf3 would hang a pawn due to ...Bxd4. White is forced to double their pawns on the kingside.} 6...Bxf3 7.gxf3 {Only move not to lose material. We'll force the bishop to make a decision.} 7...a6 8.Bxc6+ {White takes on c6, which is very logical, as to provoke doubled pawns.} 8...bxc6 {Black has sufficient counterplay with open lines for their pieces. We'll activate the knight and complete castling, as well as place our rook on the b-file. A smart pawn break at the right moment could be ...c5.} *

