[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/f4307662-b346-4aa6-8ee1-fd9ac4fd7d53/studies/f2aec3d4-cfcf-4ba4-8350-d1f63607e143/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of the first chapter! This study will cover some of the mainline with 4. Be3, as well as 4.Bf4 and 4.Bg5 for White. White moves their center pawn to e4, which is the most popular move at all levels. The Modern Defense starts with 1... g6. Black's idea is to put the Bishop on g7, and depending on how White develops, Black will combat in the center.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Our bishop will be nicely placed on g7 to attack the center from afar. Black does not have an immediate threat, so White has some flexibility in this position.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {This is the main line by White, and one of the more aggressive schemes. White's idea may be to develop the dark-squared bishop, move the queen to d2, castle long, and launch a kingside attack. For us, ...d6 is played so that our knight can hop to d7, and prepare an ...e5 or ...c5 break in the center at some point.} 3...d6 4.Bf4 {This is a move you might see White play, but it's not accurate, as the bishop on f4 can be a target for ...e5 in the future. It's played only 3% of the time. ...Nc6 attacks d4, and there are a few logical ways for White to defend.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {Nf3 is a logical attempt to defend the pawn on d4. However, White has to be careful after the following move. ...Bg4 pins the knight which is protecting d4, and capturing is an immediate threat.} 5...Bg4 6.d5 {We always have to consider what happens if White attacks the knight. We'll jump the knight forward anyway to put pressure on the pinned knight on f3!} 6...Nd4 7.Be2 {White's only move to avoid doubled pawns. We'll take the bishop pair.} 7...Nxe2 8.Qxe2 {Qxe2 makes the most sense. Black has several options from here, with a flexible position.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 150 Attack (Solid Lines) / Study 1: White Castles Short (Part 1) / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/f4307662-b346-4aa6-8ee1-fd9ac4fd7d53/studies/f2aec3d4-cfcf-4ba4-8350-d1f63607e143/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of the first chapter! This study will cover some of the mainline with 4. Be3, as well as 4.Bf4 and 4.Bg5 for White. White moves their center pawn to e4, which is the most popular move at all levels. The Modern Defense starts with 1... g6. Black's idea is to put the Bishop on g7, and depending on how White develops, Black will combat in the center.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Our bishop will be nicely placed on g7 to attack the center from afar. Black does not have an immediate threat, so White has some flexibility in this position.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {This is the main line by White, and one of the more aggressive schemes. White's idea may be to develop the dark-squared bishop, move the queen to d2, castle long, and launch a kingside attack. For us, ...d6 is played so that our knight can hop to d7, and prepare an ...e5 or ...c5 break in the center at some point.} 3...d6 4.Bg5 {This is a move we might also see, possibly with the idea to pin the e-pawn, which could be a nuisance. It's played 5% of the time. We begin our plan to expand on the queenside, especially in case White castles over there.} 4...a6 5.f4 {This is an awkward version of the Austrian Attack, except the bishop is already on g5. We can continue our queenside expansion. Our bishop is coming to b7.} 5...b5 6.Nf3 {White develops naturally. We develop as well, eyeing a central pawn.} 6...Bb7 7.Bd3 {White defends the center, so that 7... b4 does not win a pawn for Black. One of the more natural developing moves is ...Nd7. We're not yet committing our kingside knight, as the bishop on g5 might be a target.} 7...Nd7 8.e5 {White may expand in the center while taking some key squares away.} 8...c5 {We initiate contact in the center, as White's center is vulnerable. If White plays accurately, they might keep a very small edge after a move like Be4. If not, they are going to have to deal with the complicated central tension.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 150 Attack (Solid Lines) / Study 1: White Castles Short (Part 1) / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/f4307662-b346-4aa6-8ee1-fd9ac4fd7d53/studies/f2aec3d4-cfcf-4ba4-8350-d1f63607e143/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of the first chapter! This study will cover some of the mainline with 4. Be3, as well as 4.Bf4 and 4.Bg5 for White. White moves their center pawn to e4, which is the most popular move at all levels. The Modern Defense starts with 1... g6. Black's idea is to put the Bishop on g7, and depending on how White develops, Black will combat in the center.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Our bishop will be nicely placed on g7 to attack the center from afar. Black does not have an immediate threat, so White has some flexibility in this position.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {This is the main line by White, and one of the more aggressive schemes. White's idea may be to develop the dark-squared bishop, move the queen to d2, castle long, and launch a kingside attack. For us, ...d6 is played so that our knight can hop to d7, and prepare an ...e5 or ...c5 break in the center at some point.} 3...d6 4.Bg5 {This is a move we might also see, possibly with the idea to pin the e-pawn, which could be a nuisance. It's played 5% of the time. We begin our plan to expand on the queenside, especially in case White castles over there.} 4...a6 5.Qd2 {White keeps castling options flexible. We continue expanding on the queenside, as is our plan.} 5...b5 6.O-O-O {Castling queenside is White's most aggressive strategy, as they can prepare a kingside pawn assault. We develop our bishop to the long diagonal, with the intention of playing ...b4, and taking e4 if they allow it.} 6...Bb7 7.f3 {The move f3 protects the central pawn. We develop our knight, which could support multiple pawn breaks.} 7...Nd7 8.Kb1 {Kb1 is very thematic in queenside castling situations to protect the king.} 8...c5 {Not only does Black strike in the center, but also the c-pawn may support a queenside assault! The position is equal according to the computer, but things could get really dicey easily.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 150 Attack (Solid Lines) / Study 2: White Castles Short (Part 2) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/f4307662-b346-4aa6-8ee1-fd9ac4fd7d53/studies/f15aebc3-516c-4ff9-b9f4-20de50f05567/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of the first chapter! This study will look more into Be3, and the variations where White allows ...b5. Even more of these will be covered in the second chapter! White goes e4. We'll reply with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Two pawns in the center. Let's develop our bishop.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight. We'll play ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Be3 {This is White's most common move. We'll play ...a6 to support the b5-square. This study will focus on White allowing ...b5.} 4...a6 5.Qd2 {White continues with their aggressive development scheme, preparing O-O-O and Bh6 in the process. Expect to see this move more than 55% of the time. Continuing our queenside expansion.} 5...b5 6.a4 {This is a common motif by White to try and break up the queenside structure. We will push forward and attack the knight.} 6...b4 7.Nce2 {Maybe not objectively the most logical, but Qxb4 is a "threat". We can leave the pawn hanging! Taking the pawn will be a headache for White, as the queen will easily be attacked.} 7...Nd7 8.Qxb4 {We'll assume that White snacks on the pawn. ...Rb8 is a good move to get a tempo on the queen while taking the semi-open b-file.} 8...Rb8 9.Qa3 {White moves the queen away. We'll strike in the center, once again trying to open the diagonal. Since we've offered a pawn, we want to speed up our play.} 9...c5 10.c3 {c3 is very solid to defend the d4-pawn and form a pawn chain.} 10...Qb6 {Black's piece coordination comes to life, and White is super behind in development. After ...Qb6, we are putting pressure on the key weak points in White's position, such as b2 and d4. Our bishop on g7 might come to life soon.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 150 Attack (Solid Lines) / Study 2: White Castles Short (Part 2) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/f4307662-b346-4aa6-8ee1-fd9ac4fd7d53/studies/f15aebc3-516c-4ff9-b9f4-20de50f05567/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of the first chapter! This study will look more into Be3, and the variations where White allows ...b5. Even more of these will be covered in the second chapter! White goes e4. We'll reply with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Two pawns in the center. Let's develop our bishop.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight. We'll play ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Be3 {This is White's most common move. We'll play ...a6 to support the b5-square. This study will focus on White allowing ...b5.} 4...a6 5.Nf3 {Nf3 is a less-committal move by White, because f3-g4 is no longer in play. It's played 5% of the time. We will continue to expand on the queenside, and see what White does.} 5...b5 6.Bd3 {White nearly completes their development, and prepares to castle. We'll continue developing our queenside.} 6...Bb7 7.O-O {White castles. We'll develop our queenside knight, with the intention of ...c5. It is one of the most important pawn breaks, after all.} 7...Nd7 8.Qd2 {White connects their rooks, and is possibly ready to use the queen-bishop battery. We'll strike in the center, helping our dark-squared bishop open.} 8...c5 9.dxc5 {Taking is logical, as the tension can be stressful.} 9...Nxc5 {Black has a very solid position, with straightforward piece placement. We'll look to capture the bishop on d3 and finish our kingside development. Our queen can join on c7.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 150 Attack (Solid Lines) / Study 2: White Castles Short (Part 2) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/f4307662-b346-4aa6-8ee1-fd9ac4fd7d53/studies/f15aebc3-516c-4ff9-b9f4-20de50f05567/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of the first chapter! This study will look more into Be3, and the variations where White allows ...b5. Even more of these will be covered in the second chapter! White goes e4. We'll reply with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Two pawns in the center. Let's develop our bishop.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight. We'll play ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Be3 {This is White's most common move. We'll play ...a6 to support the b5-square. This study will focus on White allowing ...b5.} 4...a6 5.Nf3 {Nf3 is a less-committal move by White, because f3-g4 is no longer in play. It's played 5% of the time. We will continue to expand on the queenside, and see what White does.} 5...b5 6.a3 {a3 does not really scare us. All it does is stop b4. We'll still develop the bishop to the long diagonal and prepare queenside counterplay.} 6...Bb7 7.Bd3 {White does the same, preparing to castle. We'll develop our queenside knight, possibly preparing ...c5.} 7...Nd7 8.O-O {White chooses to castle short.} 8...Ngf6 {Our development is nearly complete, and we're prepared for counterplay. We'll castle and strike with ...c5 at the right moment. Our rook can also join the party on c8 at some point.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 150 Attack (Solid Lines) / Study 2: White Castles Short (Part 2) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/f4307662-b346-4aa6-8ee1-fd9ac4fd7d53/studies/f15aebc3-516c-4ff9-b9f4-20de50f05567/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of the first chapter! This study will look more into Be3, and the variations where White allows ...b5. Even more of these will be covered in the second chapter! White goes e4. We'll reply with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Two pawns in the center. Let's develop our bishop.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight. We'll play ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Be3 {This is White's most common move. We'll play ...a6 to support the b5-square. This study will focus on White allowing ...b5.} 4...a6 5.Qd2 {White continues with their aggressive development scheme, preparing O-O-O and Bh6 in the process. Expect to see this move more than 55% of the time. Continuing our queenside expansion.} 5...b5 6.a4 {This is a common motif by White to try and break up the queenside structure. We will push forward and attack the knight.} 6...b4 7.Nd1 {Nd1 is also very logical, this time keeping the bishop open. We'll go ahead and protect b4.} 7...a5 8.c3 {White can try to chip away at the queenside. We'll take as we can't effectively protect the pawn anymore.} 8...bxc3 9.bxc3 {White captures back with the pawn. We'll simply develop and prepare to castle, attacking the e4-pawn.} 9...Nf6 10.f3 {White defends.} 10...O-O {We'll castle, and play for ...c5 here. The position is equal, but Black has a lot of potential and easy play ahead. Some logical developing moves include ...Nd7 and ...Bb7, but you can hardly go wrong with development here.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 150 Attack (White Castles Queenside) / Study 1: White Gets Scared (a4 lines) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/dd283fea-8f7c-4190-a3ba-8ae31e76046f/studies/26906e06-36fe-4ffb-bdd0-85f077e3bb97/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of the second chapter! This chapter will focus more on the "Anti-Fianchetto" lines where White goes Be3-Qd2. This study will focus on lines where White omits aggressive h4-g4 ideas. White goes e4. We'll play ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Two pawns in the center. Let's fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {White goes for Nc3. We'll take some space with ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Be3 {White goes for the standard Be3. We'll prepare ...b5.} 4...a6 5.Qd2 {White makes a battery with the bishop and the queen. We'll continue with our queenside expansion strategy.} 5...b5 6.f3 {6.f3 delays White's commitment to how they want to approach the position and which side they want to play on. It's played 19% of the time. Black is positioned for a queenside attack, and is ready for what White has to offer.} 6...Nd7 7.a4 {White may choose to break up the structure on the queenside. We looked at that same move last chapter, but f3 was not played. No problem. We'll just kick the knight.} 7...b4 8.Nd5 {The knight may look scary on d5, but there are no concrete threats besides the b4-pawn. Which we will protect.} 8...a5 9.O-O-O {White castles long, in lieu of their lack of development on the kingside.} 9...Bb7 {We'll play ...Bb7. We have several ideas, and now that White has castled, we know where to attack! We can capture the knight on d5, finish kingside development, and eventually push with the c-pawn and strike on the queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 150 Attack (White Castles Queenside) / Study 1: White Gets Scared (a4 lines) / Variation 1"]
