1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of the second chapter! This chapter will focus more on the "Anti-Fianchetto" lines where White goes Be3-Qd2. This study will focus on lines where White omits aggressive h4-g4 ideas. White goes e4. We'll play ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Two pawns in the center. Let's fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {White goes for Nc3. We'll take some space with ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Be3 {White goes for the standard Be3. We'll prepare ...b5.} 4...a6 5.Qd2 {White makes a battery with the bishop and the queen. We'll continue with our queenside expansion strategy.} 5...b5 6.f3 {6.f3 delays White's commitment to how they want to approach the position and which side they want to play on. It's played 19% of the time. Black is positioned for a queenside attack, and is ready for what White has to offer.} 6...Nd7 7.a4 {White may choose to break up the structure on the queenside. We looked at that same move last chapter, but f3 was not played. No problem. We'll just kick the knight.} 7...b4 8.Nd1 {Nd1 indicates that White may not be all in on castling long. As we have a lead in development, we don't mind giving up b4 to gain an initiative.} 8...Ngf6 9.Qxb4 {White will likely take the pawn. And we play a move we wanted to play anyway, with tempo on the queen!} 9...c5 10.Qd2 {Retreating is very logical, as White may want to avoid risks. Black clarifies things in the center.} 10...cxd4 11.Bxd4 {White recaptures with the bishop which makes sense.} 11...O-O {Black has several ideas, and should have an interesting game from here. White still hasn't castled their king, which could prove to be a problem. We'll look to play ...e5 in the center and improve with the bishop on the diagonal and the rook on c8.} *

