1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of the second chapter! This chapter will focus more on the "Anti-Fianchetto" lines where White goes Be3-Qd2. This study will focus on lines where White omits aggressive h4-g4 ideas. White goes e4. We'll play ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Two pawns in the center. Let's fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {White goes for Nc3. We'll take some space with ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Be3 {White goes for the standard Be3. We'll prepare ...b5.} 4...a6 5.Qd2 {White makes a battery with the bishop and the queen. We'll continue with our queenside expansion strategy.} 5...b5 6.f3 {6.f3 delays White's commitment to how they want to approach the position and which side they want to play on. It's played 19% of the time. Black is positioned for a queenside attack, and is ready for what White has to offer.} 6...Nd7 7.a4 {White may choose to break up the structure on the queenside. We looked at that same move last chapter, but f3 was not played. No problem. We'll just kick the knight.} 7...b4 8.Nd1 {Nd1 indicates that White may not be all in on castling long. As we have a lead in development, we don't mind giving up b4 to gain an initiative.} 8...Ngf6 9.Qxb4 {White will likely take the pawn. And we play a move we wanted to play anyway, with tempo on the queen!} 9...c5 10.dxc5 {White may decide to get greedy.} 10...Rb8 {We can hit the queen first, and then recapture the pawn with the knight. Black is doing fine for being down a pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 150 Attack (White Castles Queenside) / Study 2: White Attacks Us (h4 g4 lines) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/dd283fea-8f7c-4190-a3ba-8ae31e76046f/studies/3c2912f8-a1d9-491b-a489-fafb593a74eb/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of chapter 2! We're going to analyze the more aggressive lines in this study, where White goes g4 and h4. It can look scary to the untrained eye, but if you study this chapter, and pay attention to the details, you should be in good shape! White plays e4, and we reply with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Two pawns in the center. Let's get our bishop to g7.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight. We'll push ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Be3 {The bishop comes out to e3, as per usual. We'll push with ...a6, potentially preparing ...b5.} 4...a6 5.Qd2 {White improves the queen. We'll expand on the queenside.} 5...b5 6.f3 {White plays f3, solidifying the center and possibly preparing g4. Whatever attack White goes for, we stay consistent with our queenside counterplay which consists of ...Nd7, ...Bb7, ...Rc8, and ...c5. The idea is to counteract O-O-O with a queenside launch of our own.} 6...Nd7 7.h4 {White is preparing for a thematic "Yugoslav" attack with h4-g4-h5, with the idea to rip open the kingside. We're going to make it difficult for White to attack by using our h-pawn to serve as a roadblock. If g4 now, we can capture.} 7...h5 8.Nh3 {When playing ...h5, we did weaken the g5-square, and White may try to utilize it. We're not really concerned, and we're going to continue with our queenside development scheme.} 8...Bb7 9.Ng5 {White hops on the g5-square. We continue to prepare our queenside counterplay.} 9...Rc8 10.O-O-O {White castles long, making their intentions clear to attack on the kingside. But we're ready to break on the queenside with ...c5!} 10...c5 11.dxc5 {White captures.} 11...Nxc5 {We'll capture with the knight to activate the piece while keeping the rook's c-file open. White will probably go Nd5, after which we can play ...Nf6 or ...e6 to trade or kick the knight away. Our queen can slide to the c-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 150 Attack (White Castles Queenside) / Study 2: White Attacks Us (h4 g4 lines) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/dd283fea-8f7c-4190-a3ba-8ae31e76046f/studies/3c2912f8-a1d9-491b-a489-fafb593a74eb/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of chapter 2! We're going to analyze the more aggressive lines in this study, where White goes g4 and h4. It can look scary to the untrained eye, but if you study this chapter, and pay attention to the details, you should be in good shape! White plays e4, and we reply with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Two pawns in the center. Let's get our bishop to g7.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight. We'll push ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Be3 {The bishop comes out to e3, as per usual. We'll push with ...a6, potentially preparing ...b5.} 4...a6 5.Qd2 {White improves the queen. We'll expand on the queenside.} 5...b5 6.f3 {White plays f3, solidifying the center and possibly preparing g4. Whatever attack White goes for, we stay consistent with our queenside counterplay which consists of ...Nd7, ...Bb7, ...Rc8, and ...c5. The idea is to counteract O-O-O with a queenside launch of our own.} 6...Nd7 7.g4 {White may start with the g-pawn, and follow it up with h4-h5. This shouldn't warrant an immediate reaction, as the g-pawn does not have an immediate "threat". We continue our queenside development.} 7...Bb7 8.h4 {White now has both pawns ready for attack. Now, we make White's attacking job more difficult. Either they allow us to take on g4, opening the h-file, they take and let our rook out, or ...g5 locks up the kingside, which favors us.} 8...h5 9.gxh5 {Taking is logical by White to provoke our rook out. Taking with the rook makes by far the most sense. Now the h4-pawn might be a target.} 9...Rxh5 10.Nge2 {White keeps their development flexible and prepares to potentially reroute their knight.} 10...e5 {Black has good counterplay with very little weaknesses to show for it. The position is equal, but White has to worry about a lot of things after ...e5. There is tension in the center and the h4-pawn is under attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 150 Attack (White Castles Queenside) / Study 2: White Attacks Us (h4 g4 lines) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/dd283fea-8f7c-4190-a3ba-8ae31e76046f/studies/3c2912f8-a1d9-491b-a489-fafb593a74eb/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of chapter 2! We're going to analyze the more aggressive lines in this study, where White goes g4 and h4. It can look scary to the untrained eye, but if you study this chapter, and pay attention to the details, you should be in good shape! White plays e4, and we reply with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Two pawns in the center. Let's get our bishop to g7.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight. We'll push ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Be3 {The bishop comes out to e3, as per usual. We'll push with ...a6, potentially preparing ...b5.} 4...a6 5.Qd2 {White improves the queen. We'll expand on the queenside.} 5...b5 6.f3 {White plays f3, solidifying the center and possibly preparing g4. Whatever attack White goes for, we stay consistent with our queenside counterplay which consists of ...Nd7, ...Bb7, ...Rc8, and ...c5. The idea is to counteract O-O-O with a queenside launch of our own.} 6...Nd7 7.g4 {White may start with the g-pawn, and follow it up with h4-h5. This shouldn't warrant an immediate reaction, as the g-pawn does not have an immediate "threat". We continue our queenside development.} 7...Bb7 8.h4 {White now has both pawns ready for attack. Now, we make White's attacking job more difficult. Either they allow us to take on g4, opening the h-file, they take and let our rook out, or ...g5 locks up the kingside, which favors us.} 8...h5 9.g5 {This closes up the position, which is what we want. Now that White took away the f6-square from our knight, we should play ...e6 to develop the knight to e7.} 9...e6 10.Nge2 {White also develops the knight. We follow through.} 10...Ne7 11.Ng3 {White places their knight on g3, possibly positioned for an f4-f5 break.} 11...d5 {We're going to be able to undermine White's position, as White has left some weaknesses. Pushing the f-pawn might not be the smartest decision now by White. We should consider ...Rc8 and castling.} *

