1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of chapter 2! We're going to analyze the more aggressive lines in this study, where White goes g4 and h4. It can look scary to the untrained eye, but if you study this chapter, and pay attention to the details, you should be in good shape! White plays e4, and we reply with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Two pawns in the center. Let's get our bishop to g7.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight. We'll push ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Be3 {The bishop comes out to e3, as per usual. We'll push with ...a6, potentially preparing ...b5.} 4...a6 5.Qd2 {White improves the queen. We'll expand on the queenside.} 5...b5 6.f3 {White plays f3, solidifying the center and possibly preparing g4. Whatever attack White goes for, we stay consistent with our queenside counterplay which consists of ...Nd7, ...Bb7, ...Rc8, and ...c5. The idea is to counteract O-O-O with a queenside launch of our own.} 6...Nd7 7.O-O-O {If White castles queenside before committing to a kingside pawn storm, we're not worried. We continue our queenside development.} 7...Bb7 8.g4 {This doesn't faze us.} 8...Rc8 {We'll play ...Rc8, and the ...c5 thrust is coming next. Our counterplay in the center will be much quicker than White's attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The 150 Attack (White Castles Queenside) / Study 2: White Attacks Us (h4 g4 lines) / Variation 5"]
