1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of chapter 2! We're going to analyze the more aggressive lines in this study, where White goes g4 and h4. It can look scary to the untrained eye, but if you study this chapter, and pay attention to the details, you should be in good shape! White plays e4, and we reply with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Two pawns in the center. Let's get our bishop to g7.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight. We'll push ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Be3 {The bishop comes out to e3, as per usual. We'll push with ...a6, potentially preparing ...b5.} 4...a6 5.Qd2 {White improves the queen. We'll expand on the queenside.} 5...b5 6.h4 {White may also go for h4 immediately, making their intentions of a kingside attack clear. This move is played 7% of the time. We'll play ...h6 with an idea in mind.} 6...h6 7.h5 {White barges forward anyway.} 7...g5 {But we lock the center up with ...g5. Now it's going to be much harder for White to attack. We'll continue standard development from here.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Austrian Attack (e4, d4, f4) / Study 1: White Plays 5.Nf3 (All Lines) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/3ebfd3fa-ca05-4823-bc5d-64ed5fb5f84c/studies/8d64890b-db77-4046-b73e-daf719b3800a/lines"]

1.e4 {This chapter will cover the Austrian Attack, where White lunges three of their pawns (d, e, and f) forward. Not only is it one of the more aggressive lines, but it gains a lot of space for White. This study will focus on the very natural, but inaccurate 5.Nf3, which can be exploited by a pin. White goes for e4. We play ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White plays d4. We'll complete the fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {The knight comes out to c3. It's time for us to play ...d6.} 3...d6 4.f4 {The Austrian Attack is symbolized by White placing all three of their pawns in the center, grabbing as much space as possible. It's all about central control. It's played 15% of the time on average on the amateur level. The idea behind ...Nc6 is that we're immediately pressuring the d4-pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {While this is a very natural developing move, Nf3 actually plays right into our hands. And it's played 73% of the time! We'll pin the knight which defends d4, forcing White to react.} 5...Bg4 6.Be3 {Be3 is the most "thematic" move in the position, not forcing anything, while defending d4 with a piece. We're still going to take on f3, as gxf3 is forced.} 6...Bxf3 7.gxf3 {White's pawn structure is slightly fractured. This looks like a move you shouldn't play, as White has two ways to capture. However, both captures have consequences!} 7...d5 8.exd5 {Taking with the pawn is the most forceful, as the c6-knight is attacked. But we'll be able to recoup the pawn in this case.} 8...Nb4 9.Bb5+ {White may give us a check. It's tricky to block.} 9...Kf8 {No need to fear, ...Kf8 is totally fine! We will be able to recover d5 and hold a grip on the light squares. White cannot attack our king anymore, and we'll maneuver our kingside knight to f5 via h6.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Austrian Attack (e4, d4, f4) / Study 1: White Plays 5.Nf3 (All Lines) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/3ebfd3fa-ca05-4823-bc5d-64ed5fb5f84c/studies/8d64890b-db77-4046-b73e-daf719b3800a/lines"]

1.e4 {This chapter will cover the Austrian Attack, where White lunges three of their pawns (d, e, and f) forward. Not only is it one of the more aggressive lines, but it gains a lot of space for White. This study will focus on the very natural, but inaccurate 5.Nf3, which can be exploited by a pin. White goes for e4. We play ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White plays d4. We'll complete the fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {The knight comes out to c3. It's time for us to play ...d6.} 3...d6 4.f4 {The Austrian Attack is symbolized by White placing all three of their pawns in the center, grabbing as much space as possible. It's all about central control. It's played 15% of the time on average on the amateur level. The idea behind ...Nc6 is that we're immediately pressuring the d4-pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {While this is a very natural developing move, Nf3 actually plays right into our hands. And it's played 73% of the time! We'll pin the knight which defends d4, forcing White to react.} 5...Bg4 6.Be3 {Be3 is the most "thematic" move in the position, not forcing anything, while defending d4 with a piece. We're still going to take on f3, as gxf3 is forced.} 6...Bxf3 7.gxf3 {White's pawn structure is slightly fractured. This looks like a move you shouldn't play, as White has two ways to capture. However, both captures have consequences!} 7...d5 8.Nxd5 {Nxd5 looks good, as White captures a pawn without damaging their pawn structure. However, it's actually a mistake. We kick the knight away, and now the d4-pawn is ours.} 8...e6 9.Nc3 {White retreats.} 9...Bxd4 {With the possible absence of White's dark squared Bishop, ...Qh4+ is suddenly very scary for White. And we are already pressuring their dark-squared bishop which usually defends on f2.} *

