1.e4 {This chapter will cover the Austrian Attack, where White lunges three of their pawns (d, e, and f) forward. Not only is it one of the more aggressive lines, but it gains a lot of space for White. This study will focus on the very natural, but inaccurate 5.Nf3, which can be exploited by a pin. White goes for e4. We play ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White plays d4. We'll complete the fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {The knight comes out to c3. It's time for us to play ...d6.} 3...d6 4.f4 {The Austrian Attack is symbolized by White placing all three of their pawns in the center, grabbing as much space as possible. It's all about central control. It's played 15% of the time on average on the amateur level. The idea behind ...Nc6 is that we're immediately pressuring the d4-pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {While this is a very natural developing move, Nf3 actually plays right into our hands. And it's played 73% of the time! We'll pin the knight which defends d4, forcing White to react.} 5...Bg4 6.Be3 {Be3 is the most "thematic" move in the position, not forcing anything, while defending d4 with a piece. We're still going to take on f3, as gxf3 is forced.} 6...Bxf3 7.gxf3 {White's pawn structure is slightly fractured. This looks like a move you shouldn't play, as White has two ways to capture. However, both captures have consequences!} 7...d5 8.e5 {Advancing the pawn is another try by White to take some central space and restrict our bishop.} 8...Nh6 {But ...Nh6 is thematic to hop into f5 next, exploiting the light-squared weaknesses just created. This is not the best approach by White.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Austrian Attack (e4, d4, f4) / Study 1: White Plays 5.Nf3 (All Lines) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/3ebfd3fa-ca05-4823-bc5d-64ed5fb5f84c/studies/8d64890b-db77-4046-b73e-daf719b3800a/lines"]

1.e4 {This chapter will cover the Austrian Attack, where White lunges three of their pawns (d, e, and f) forward. Not only is it one of the more aggressive lines, but it gains a lot of space for White. This study will focus on the very natural, but inaccurate 5.Nf3, which can be exploited by a pin. White goes for e4. We play ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White plays d4. We'll complete the fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {The knight comes out to c3. It's time for us to play ...d6.} 3...d6 4.f4 {The Austrian Attack is symbolized by White placing all three of their pawns in the center, grabbing as much space as possible. It's all about central control. It's played 15% of the time on average on the amateur level. The idea behind ...Nc6 is that we're immediately pressuring the d4-pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {While this is a very natural developing move, Nf3 actually plays right into our hands. And it's played 73% of the time! We'll pin the knight which defends d4, forcing White to react.} 5...Bg4 6.Be3 {Be3 is the most "thematic" move in the position, not forcing anything, while defending d4 with a piece. We're still going to take on f3, as gxf3 is forced.} 6...Bxf3 7.gxf3 {White's pawn structure is slightly fractured. This looks like a move you shouldn't play, as White has two ways to capture. However, both captures have consequences!} 7...d5 8.Qd2 {White should just ignore d5, and continue with their development. We're going to solidify d5, and develop normally.} 8...e6 9.O-O-O {White completes their queenside development and castles.} 9...Nge7 {We have a solid position, and can long term try to exploit the doubled pawns.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Austrian Attack (e4, d4, f4) / Study 1: White Plays 5.Nf3 (All Lines) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/3ebfd3fa-ca05-4823-bc5d-64ed5fb5f84c/studies/8d64890b-db77-4046-b73e-daf719b3800a/lines"]

1.e4 {This chapter will cover the Austrian Attack, where White lunges three of their pawns (d, e, and f) forward. Not only is it one of the more aggressive lines, but it gains a lot of space for White. This study will focus on the very natural, but inaccurate 5.Nf3, which can be exploited by a pin. White goes for e4. We play ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White plays d4. We'll complete the fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {The knight comes out to c3. It's time for us to play ...d6.} 3...d6 4.f4 {The Austrian Attack is symbolized by White placing all three of their pawns in the center, grabbing as much space as possible. It's all about central control. It's played 15% of the time on average on the amateur level. The idea behind ...Nc6 is that we're immediately pressuring the d4-pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {While this is a very natural developing move, Nf3 actually plays right into our hands. And it's played 73% of the time! We'll pin the knight which defends d4, forcing White to react.} 5...Bg4 6.d5 {White may move the pawn forward to attack the knight. The following response is pretty thematic in chapter 7, where White goes for d4-e4-c4 structure.} 6...Nd4 {Exploiting the pin, so that capturing the knight on this turn is not possible.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Austrian Attack (e4, d4, f4) / Study 1: White Plays 5.Nf3 (All Lines) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/3ebfd3fa-ca05-4823-bc5d-64ed5fb5f84c/studies/8d64890b-db77-4046-b73e-daf719b3800a/lines"]

1.e4 {This chapter will cover the Austrian Attack, where White lunges three of their pawns (d, e, and f) forward. Not only is it one of the more aggressive lines, but it gains a lot of space for White. This study will focus on the very natural, but inaccurate 5.Nf3, which can be exploited by a pin. White goes for e4. We play ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White plays d4. We'll complete the fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {The knight comes out to c3. It's time for us to play ...d6.} 3...d6 4.f4 {The Austrian Attack is symbolized by White placing all three of their pawns in the center, grabbing as much space as possible. It's all about central control. It's played 15% of the time on average on the amateur level. The idea behind ...Nc6 is that we're immediately pressuring the d4-pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {While this is a very natural developing move, Nf3 actually plays right into our hands. And it's played 73% of the time! We'll pin the knight which defends d4, forcing White to react.} 5...Bg4 6.Bb5 {White might counterpin our knight on c6. But we can still take on f3. White can't recapture with the queen because our bishop is still attacking the d4-pawn.} 6...Bxf3 7.gxf3 {Saddling White with doubled pawns.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Austrian Attack (e4, d4, f4) / Study 2: Other 5th Moves / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/3ebfd3fa-ca05-4823-bc5d-64ed5fb5f84c/studies/514cc513-ebce-4dad-bf3c-25e2077c1899/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of our chapter on the Austrian Attack. This study will focus on other 5th moves for White, so that you can be prepared for anything! White goes for e4. Let's play the Modern.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Both pawns in the center. We'll develop our bishop first.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {The knight is out. We'll take away some squares from White.} 3...d6 4.f4 {The Austrian Attack. White is aggressive with three pawns controlling the center. Let's attack the d4-pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Be3 {Be3 is the best way for White to defend d4, and it's played 13% of the time here. In the previous study, the knight was on f3, so we could play ...Bg4, continuing the pressure on d4. Here, we don't have such a luxury. We're not going to go for anything extravagant. The point is to remain solid and wait for White to provoke weaknesses.} 5...Nf6 6.Nf3 {We should assume that White doesn't make mistakes, and chooses to develop and castle. We'll do the same.} 6...O-O 7.Be2 {White follows suit. We'll play ...e6 to keep our pawns flexible, as we might want to go for ...d5.} 7...e6 8.O-O {White castles short.} 8...b6 {We're still going to stay flexible, while controlling the light squares more. Our bishop is coming to b7 and the knight will reroute to e7. Eventually, we can look to strike in the center with a move like ...c5.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Austrian Attack (e4, d4, f4) / Study 2: Other 5th Moves / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/3ebfd3fa-ca05-4823-bc5d-64ed5fb5f84c/studies/514cc513-ebce-4dad-bf3c-25e2077c1899/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of our chapter on the Austrian Attack. This study will focus on other 5th moves for White, so that you can be prepared for anything! White goes for e4. Let's play the Modern.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Both pawns in the center. We'll develop our bishop first.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {The knight is out. We'll take away some squares from White.} 3...d6 4.f4 {The Austrian Attack. White is aggressive with three pawns controlling the center. Let's attack the d4-pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Bb5 {Although played only 2% of the time, Bb5 is a logical try to pin our knight and defend the pawn that way. We'll attack the bishop, prompting Bxc6.} 5...a6 6.Bxc6+ {White takes on c6 to avoid losing anything. We capture back.} 6...bxc6 7.Nf3 {White at this point likely commits to developing and castling short. We'll do the same.} 7...Nf6 8.O-O {White castles.} 8...O-O {As do we!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Austrian Attack (e4, d4, f4) / Study 2: Other 5th Moves / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/3ebfd3fa-ca05-4823-bc5d-64ed5fb5f84c/studies/514cc513-ebce-4dad-bf3c-25e2077c1899/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of our chapter on the Austrian Attack. This study will focus on other 5th moves for White, so that you can be prepared for anything! White goes for e4. Let's play the Modern.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Both pawns in the center. We'll develop our bishop first.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {The knight is out. We'll take away some squares from White.} 3...d6 4.f4 {The Austrian Attack. White is aggressive with three pawns controlling the center. Let's attack the d4-pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.Bb5 {Although played only 2% of the time, Bb5 is a logical try to pin our knight and defend the pawn that way. We'll attack the bishop, prompting Bxc6.} 5...a6 6.Ba4 {Retreating seems logical to maintain the pin. However, after ...b5 we'll be able to win our pawn.} 6...b5 7.d5 {Unless White finds d5. Stockfish gives ...Na5 as the best move here, however, taking on a4 also has a cool idea to it. We can allow the trade and take.} 7...bxa4 8.dxc6 {White must recover their material.} 8...a3 {But we utilize our bishop's placement as White can't take on a3. Long-term, we eliminate one of our doubled pawns and c6 can be a weakness. Black is doing great and already has an advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Austrian Attack (e4, d4, f4) / Study 2: Other 5th Moves / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/3ebfd3fa-ca05-4823-bc5d-64ed5fb5f84c/studies/514cc513-ebce-4dad-bf3c-25e2077c1899/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of our chapter on the Austrian Attack. This study will focus on other 5th moves for White, so that you can be prepared for anything! White goes for e4. Let's play the Modern.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Both pawns in the center. We'll develop our bishop first.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {The knight is out. We'll take away some squares from White.} 3...d6 4.f4 {The Austrian Attack. White is aggressive with three pawns controlling the center. Let's attack the d4-pawn.} 4...Nc6 5.d5 {White attacking the knight is something to consider, and it's played 6% of the time. This attacks the knight but possibly overextends the structure and opens up our bishop. We can generally meet d5 with ...Nd4, as the bishop nicely protects the knight.} 5...Nd4 6.Be3 {White develops a piece while attacking the knight. We can protect with a pawn, utilizing our ...c5 break defensively this time.} 6...c5 7.dxc6 {En passant does not faze us.} 7...Nxc6 {We recapture with the knight, and will continue our development in a standard manner. The knight will come to f6 and we will castle short. We've successfully removed one of White's most important pawns from the center.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Moves The Light Squared Bishop / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/85396ea4-50da-4704-90d5-e5362794d9b3/studies/22e34d06-0243-4907-8177-e795402bd7b0/lines"]

1.e4 {In this chapter, we will cover White going for the two central pawns followed by the two knights to f3 and c3. It's a flexible setup with a lot of central presence. In this study, we will look at White developing the light-squared bishop immediately after the knights. White plays e4, and we go ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Two pawns on e4 and d4 by White. Let's activate our bishop.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {White goes for Nc3. We'll continue with ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Nf3 {White prefers to take this approach, controlling the center, and developing both knights 30% of the time. We don't know where White is going to develop next, so it's important to stay flexible with our development and adapt accordingly. We continue with the start of our queenside expansion.} 4...a6 5.Be2 {Developing the bishop to e2 makes more sense, as White can castle next move, and the bishop is not a target for attack. Although it's only played 6% of the time. We'll develop normally.} 5...Nf6 6.O-O {White castles. So do we!} 6...O-O 7.Re1 {White patiently puts their rook in the center. We develop the knight normally, possibly preparing ...e5.} 7...Nc6 8.d5 {White attacks the knight, so where do we go? ...Na7 is not as bas as it looks. We will still be able to play on the queenside, and the knight will come back to life.} 8...Na7 9.h3 {If White just waits around...} 9...b5 {Our queenside expansion continues, and our knight will be able to come to life in a number of ways. ...Nc8-Nb6 is one way. Also, the knight does support ...c6 for now, enabling us to push and possibly recapture with it.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Moves The Light Squared Bishop / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/85396ea4-50da-4704-90d5-e5362794d9b3/studies/22e34d06-0243-4907-8177-e795402bd7b0/lines"]

1.e4 {In this chapter, we will cover White going for the two central pawns followed by the two knights to f3 and c3. It's a flexible setup with a lot of central presence. In this study, we will look at White developing the light-squared bishop immediately after the knights. White plays e4, and we go ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Two pawns on e4 and d4 by White. Let's activate our bishop.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {White goes for Nc3. We'll continue with ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Nf3 {White prefers to take this approach, controlling the center, and developing both knights 30% of the time. We don't know where White is going to develop next, so it's important to stay flexible with our development and adapt accordingly. We continue with the start of our queenside expansion.} 4...a6 5.Be2 {Developing the bishop to e2 makes more sense, as White can castle next move, and the bishop is not a target for attack. Although it's only played 6% of the time. We'll develop normally.} 5...Nf6 6.O-O {White castles. So do we!} 6...O-O 7.Re1 {White patiently puts their rook in the center. We develop the knight normally, possibly preparing ...e5.} 7...Nc6 8.h3 {If White just waits around, we can get our pawn break. Part of the purpose of ...Nc6.} 8...e5 9.d5 {Our knight is prompted to move.} 9...Ne7 {We'll retreat to e7. We have some thematic plans with ...Ne8, ...f5, and maybe ...c6-b5 to still chip away at the queenside. Black is doing well.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Moves The Light Squared Bishop / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/85396ea4-50da-4704-90d5-e5362794d9b3/studies/22e34d06-0243-4907-8177-e795402bd7b0/lines"]

1.e4 {In this chapter, we will cover White going for the two central pawns followed by the two knights to f3 and c3. It's a flexible setup with a lot of central presence. In this study, we will look at White developing the light-squared bishop immediately after the knights. White plays e4, and we go ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Two pawns on e4 and d4 by White. Let's activate our bishop.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {White goes for Nc3. We'll continue with ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Nf3 {White prefers to take this approach, controlling the center, and developing both knights 30% of the time. We don't know where White is going to develop next, so it's important to stay flexible with our development and adapt accordingly. We continue with the start of our queenside expansion.} 4...a6 5.Bc4 {Chosen 21% of the time, this is a very classical approach by White, developing both knights followed by the bishop on c4. However, Bc4 plays right into our hands. We get to play ...b5, a move we wanted to play anyway, with tempo.} 5...b5 6.Bb3 {Makes sense for White to retreat the bishop while maintaining the diagonal pointing at f7. With ...e6, we stay flexible with our structure, while blunting the bishop.} 6...e6 7.O-O {White castles to safety. We'll develop our queenside normally. Now ...b4 followed by taking on e4 is an idea.} 7...Bb7 8.Bg5 {White chooses to develop with tempo in this variation, which does not faze us. We're fine blocking with the knight.} 8...Ne7 9.Re1 {White defends e4. We can attack the bishop that moved to g5.} 9...h6 10.Bh4 {Retreating and maintaining the pin makes sense.} 10...Nd7 {Black employs a Hippo-like formation, where White has a space advantage, but has to be confrontational in order to gain anything from the position. We'll castle and then play for ...c5, supported by our rook which will come to c8.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Moves The Light Squared Bishop / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/85396ea4-50da-4704-90d5-e5362794d9b3/studies/22e34d06-0243-4907-8177-e795402bd7b0/lines"]

1.e4 {In this chapter, we will cover White going for the two central pawns followed by the two knights to f3 and c3. It's a flexible setup with a lot of central presence. In this study, we will look at White developing the light-squared bishop immediately after the knights. White plays e4, and we go ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Two pawns on e4 and d4 by White. Let's activate our bishop.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {White goes for Nc3. We'll continue with ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Nf3 {White prefers to take this approach, controlling the center, and developing both knights 30% of the time. We don't know where White is going to develop next, so it's important to stay flexible with our development and adapt accordingly. We continue with the start of our queenside expansion.} 4...a6 5.Bd3 {This is objectively the least accurate of all the light squared Bishop moves, as now the bishop blocks the queen's protection of the d4-pawn. Also, the bishop on d3 is bad against our pawn structure on f7, g6 and h7. This move is played 15% of the time. We can immediately try to exploit White's weakness!} 5...Bg4 6.h3 {While natural, this blunders a pawn! We'll take the knight.} 6...Bxf3 7.Qxf3 {Whichever way White captures...} 7...Bxd4 {We snag a pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Moves The Light Squared Bishop / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/85396ea4-50da-4704-90d5-e5362794d9b3/studies/22e34d06-0243-4907-8177-e795402bd7b0/lines"]

1.e4 {In this chapter, we will cover White going for the two central pawns followed by the two knights to f3 and c3. It's a flexible setup with a lot of central presence. In this study, we will look at White developing the light-squared bishop immediately after the knights. White plays e4, and we go ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Two pawns on e4 and d4 by White. Let's activate our bishop.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {White goes for Nc3. We'll continue with ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Nf3 {White prefers to take this approach, controlling the center, and developing both knights 30% of the time. We don't know where White is going to develop next, so it's important to stay flexible with our development and adapt accordingly. We continue with the start of our queenside expansion.} 4...a6 5.Bd3 {This is objectively the least accurate of all the light squared Bishop moves, as now the bishop blocks the queen's protection of the d4-pawn. Also, the bishop on d3 is bad against our pawn structure on f7, g6 and h7. This move is played 15% of the time. We can immediately try to exploit White's weakness!} 5...Bg4 6.Be3 {6.Be3 sufficiently protects d4.} 6...Nc6 {But we continue pressuring the center, like we saw in the 150 Attack. The d4-pawn is a huge target and we might be preparing ...e5 soon.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Moves The Light Squared Bishop / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/85396ea4-50da-4704-90d5-e5362794d9b3/studies/22e34d06-0243-4907-8177-e795402bd7b0/lines"]

1.e4 {In this chapter, we will cover White going for the two central pawns followed by the two knights to f3 and c3. It's a flexible setup with a lot of central presence. In this study, we will look at White developing the light-squared bishop immediately after the knights. White plays e4, and we go ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Two pawns on e4 and d4 by White. Let's activate our bishop.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {White goes for Nc3. We'll continue with ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Nf3 {White prefers to take this approach, controlling the center, and developing both knights 30% of the time. We don't know where White is going to develop next, so it's important to stay flexible with our development and adapt accordingly. We continue with the start of our queenside expansion.} 4...a6 5.Bc4 {Chosen 21% of the time, this is a very classical approach by White, developing both knights followed by the bishop on c4. However, Bc4 plays right into our hands. We get to play ...b5, a move we wanted to play anyway, with tempo.} 5...b5 6.Bd5 {You might encounter this move, threatening the rook.} 6...c6 {No harm done. Once White retreats, we will finish kingside development and castle.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.a4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/85396ea4-50da-4704-90d5-e5362794d9b3/studies/99bed8c5-178b-402e-8856-feb31e8fc412/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of chapter 4! This study will focus on 5. a4 by White, which is a common move to stop our ...b5 advance. White starts with e4. Let's respond with the Modern.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {Developing the queenside knight. Let's play ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Nf3 {White plays the Two Knights approach. We'll go for our standard ...a6 idea.} 4...a6 5.a4 {This is a very direct approach by White to prevent b5, although it's only played 5% of the time. Since we can't play ...b5, we will go for ...b6 and implement a Hippo setup. It's characterized by the pawns on the sixth rank, both bishops fianchettoed, and the knights on e7 and d7.} 5...b6 6.Be2 {Just like we analyzed last study, a completely acceptable way to develop. Our Hippo continues.} 6...e6 7.O-O {White castles. Where do our knights go?} 7...Nd7 8.Bg5 {Our queen is attacked. No problem... we wanted to play Ne7 anyway!} 8...Ne7 9.Qd2 {White prepares Bh6. We won't let them!} 9...h6 10.Be3 {White retreats. Our Hippo is complete!} 10...Bb7 11.Rad1 {White centralizes. This is a common motif in the Hippo, to prompt White to make a decision with the e-pawn.} 11...Nf6 12.e5 {White can attack, but leaves behind some weaknesses.} 12...Nfd5 {Black's pieces start to come to life! Our knights protect each other, and we have straightforward plans of attacking on the kingside now. We want to push ...g5 and grab even more space!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.a4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/85396ea4-50da-4704-90d5-e5362794d9b3/studies/99bed8c5-178b-402e-8856-feb31e8fc412/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of chapter 4! This study will focus on 5. a4 by White, which is a common move to stop our ...b5 advance. White starts with e4. Let's respond with the Modern.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {Developing the queenside knight. Let's play ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Nf3 {White plays the Two Knights approach. We'll go for our standard ...a6 idea.} 4...a6 5.a4 {This is a very direct approach by White to prevent b5, although it's only played 5% of the time. Since we can't play ...b5, we will go for ...b6 and implement a Hippo setup. It's characterized by the pawns on the sixth rank, both bishops fianchettoed, and the knights on e7 and d7.} 5...b6 6.Bc4 {Bc4 is now more viable because ...b5 isn't an idea. We continue with our Hippo setup, while restricting the bishop.} 6...e6 7.O-O {White castles. We'll develop our knight to e7, Hippo style. We didn't want to commit to f6 yet.} 7...Ne7 8.Be3 {White also makes natural developing moves. Now we have both knights placed on the 7th rank, ready to support pawn breaks and pounce on weaknesses.} 8...Nd7 9.Qd2 {White is trying to get Bh6 in, which would aid a potential attack. Which we will easily stop. Our bishop is too valuable to trade like this.} 9...h6 10.d5 {White finally cracks in the center, and we have a nice plan from here. Advancing our center pawn now that White has committed. This does close our bishop for the moment, but you will see our plan soon.} 10...e5 11.Ne1 {White possibly wants to reroute.} 11...f5 {This is our plan! We want to continue the aggression with ...f4 and ...g5 and attack on the kingside. This is why we closed the center in the first place. We have an extremely strong grip on the dark squares now.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 2: White Plays 5.a4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/85396ea4-50da-4704-90d5-e5362794d9b3/studies/99bed8c5-178b-402e-8856-feb31e8fc412/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of chapter 4! This study will focus on 5. a4 by White, which is a common move to stop our ...b5 advance. White starts with e4. Let's respond with the Modern.} 1...g6 2.d4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {Developing the queenside knight. Let's play ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Nf3 {White plays the Two Knights approach. We'll go for our standard ...a6 idea.} 4...a6 5.a4 {This is a very direct approach by White to prevent b5, although it's only played 5% of the time. Since we can't play ...b5, we will go for ...b6 and implement a Hippo setup. It's characterized by the pawns on the sixth rank, both bishops fianchettoed, and the knights on e7 and d7.} 5...b6 6.Bd3 {Sort of like in the previous study, Bd3 is not all that accurate, as d4 is more loose. We'll attack the center, taking advantage of White's setup.} 6...Bg4 7.Be3 {White defends d4...} 7...e6 {And we'll likely continue with our Hippo setup. The knights will jump to d7 and e7, and we'll castle. In general, the Hippo is a bit more passive but an extremely solid setup that allows you to provoke a mistake from your opponent.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 3: White Plays 5.Be3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/85396ea4-50da-4704-90d5-e5362794d9b3/studies/488cee75-6c92-4824-bee5-25d588166514/lines"]

1.e4 {We are in the final study of Chapter 4. The last two studies focused on an early light-squared bishop development. This study will focus on an early Be3, which is also thematic in a lot of Modern positions. White opens with e4, and we will reply with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White places both pawns in the center. We should fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {One knight is developed. Let's go for ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Nf3 {Both knights out now. We'll play the standard ...a6.} 4...a6 5.Be3 {White chooses 5.Be3 almost 30% of the time, making it the most popular move here. This is very similar conceptually to a 150-Attack, but there are no f3-g4 ideas, so it's less dangerous. We should continue with our usual queenside expansion.} 5...b5 6.Bd3 {White develops normally. As do we... on the queenside.} 6...Bb7 7.O-O {White castles. Once again, note that we're going all in on the queenside before committing to our kingside development. It's paramount to set up our ...c5 break as soon as possible, before White gets too much development and space.} 7...Nd7 8.Re1 {Good centralizing move. We'll strike in the center!} 8...c5 9.Qd2 {White may just wait around. We'll take in the center.} 9...cxd4 10.Nxd4 {White recaptures.} 10...Ngf6 {We'll just play chess from here. We'll castle and bring the rook to c8. Our knight on d7 is flexible to improve to either e5 or c5.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 3: White Plays 5.Be3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/85396ea4-50da-4704-90d5-e5362794d9b3/studies/488cee75-6c92-4824-bee5-25d588166514/lines"]

1.e4 {We are in the final study of Chapter 4. The last two studies focused on an early light-squared bishop development. This study will focus on an early Be3, which is also thematic in a lot of Modern positions. White opens with e4, and we will reply with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White places both pawns in the center. We should fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {One knight is developed. Let's go for ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Nf3 {Both knights out now. We'll play the standard ...a6.} 4...a6 5.Be3 {White chooses 5.Be3 almost 30% of the time, making it the most popular move here. This is very similar conceptually to a 150-Attack, but there are no f3-g4 ideas, so it's less dangerous. We should continue with our usual queenside expansion.} 5...b5 6.Bd3 {White develops normally. As do we... on the queenside.} 6...Bb7 7.O-O {White castles. Once again, note that we're going all in on the queenside before committing to our kingside development. It's paramount to set up our ...c5 break as soon as possible, before White gets too much development and space.} 7...Nd7 8.Re1 {Good centralizing move. We'll strike in the center!} 8...c5 9.dxc5 {Capturing our pawn only favors us.} 9...dxc5 {Black has a nice position. The pawn structure on the queenside is a big asset.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 3: White Plays 5.Be3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/85396ea4-50da-4704-90d5-e5362794d9b3/studies/488cee75-6c92-4824-bee5-25d588166514/lines"]

1.e4 {We are in the final study of Chapter 4. The last two studies focused on an early light-squared bishop development. This study will focus on an early Be3, which is also thematic in a lot of Modern positions. White opens with e4, and we will reply with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White places both pawns in the center. We should fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {One knight is developed. Let's go for ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Nf3 {Both knights out now. We'll play the standard ...a6.} 4...a6 5.Be3 {White chooses 5.Be3 almost 30% of the time, making it the most popular move here. This is very similar conceptually to a 150-Attack, but there are no f3-g4 ideas, so it's less dangerous. We should continue with our usual queenside expansion.} 5...b5 6.Bd3 {White develops normally. As do we... on the queenside.} 6...Bb7 7.O-O {White castles. Once again, note that we're going all in on the queenside before committing to our kingside development. It's paramount to set up our ...c5 break as soon as possible, before White gets too much development and space.} 7...Nd7 8.Re1 {Good centralizing move. We'll strike in the center!} 8...c5 9.a4 {A thematic try by White to chip away at our structure.} 9...b4 {But we'll kick the knight first. Once White retreats, we'll quickly get the knight out and prepare castling. To stabilize our queenside structure even more, our queen will join on c7. In general, our position is harmonious.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Nf3 Setups / Study 1: White Plays 3.Nf3 and 4.Bc4 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/2f8ce20e-9372-4c12-9f3d-c1d87a62dcf2/studies/a22fcee2-2b53-441d-b87f-502893867fb4/lines"]

1.e4 {Chapter 5 will focus on White developing with Nf3, but not Nc3. This study will focus on the combination between Nf3 and Bc4, and we will use ...e5 as one of the main central counterpushes in this study. White opens with e4 and we will play the Modern.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White places two pawns in the center. We'll fianchetto our bishop.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {White activates the kingside knight 34% of the time in this position, making it the most popular move. With this setup, White usually wants to leave their options open for the c-pawn to move. We'll respond with ...d6 anyway.} 3...d6 4.Bc4 {Nf3-Bc4 is a commonplace setup in chess, and it's played 17% of the time here. We shouldn't play ...Bg4 here because of sacrificial ideas on f7. We're going to continue pressuring the center instead, with ...e5 in the future.} 4...Nc6 5.O-O {White castles, which is completely logical. By playing ...Nf6, we prepare ...Bg4 since the bishop will be protected. We also attack the e4-pawn.} 5...Nf6 6.e5 {This push does not really faze us. We'll have to trade queens, but it's a fine position for us as we'll win the e5-pawn. We'll take the pawn.} 6...dxe5 7.dxe5 {White takes back. We'll trade.} 7...Qxd1 8.Rxd1 {White has to recapture} 8...Ng4 {The pawn is attacked three times and only defended once. So we'll be up a pawn. Best resistance for White is to play e6 and force us to damage our structure, but we'll be up a pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Nf3 Setups / Study 1: White Plays 3.Nf3 and 4.Bc4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/2f8ce20e-9372-4c12-9f3d-c1d87a62dcf2/studies/a22fcee2-2b53-441d-b87f-502893867fb4/lines"]

1.e4 {Chapter 5 will focus on White developing with Nf3, but not Nc3. This study will focus on the combination between Nf3 and Bc4, and we will use ...e5 as one of the main central counterpushes in this study. White opens with e4 and we will play the Modern.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White places two pawns in the center. We'll fianchetto our bishop.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {White activates the kingside knight 34% of the time in this position, making it the most popular move. With this setup, White usually wants to leave their options open for the c-pawn to move. We'll respond with ...d6 anyway.} 3...d6 4.Bc4 {Nf3-Bc4 is a commonplace setup in chess, and it's played 17% of the time here. We shouldn't play ...Bg4 here because of sacrificial ideas on f7. We're going to continue pressuring the center instead, with ...e5 in the future.} 4...Nc6 5.O-O {White castles, which is completely logical. By playing ...Nf6, we prepare ...Bg4 since the bishop will be protected. We also attack the e4-pawn.} 5...Nf6 6.Re1 {White can defend on e4 using the rook. We'll castle for now. We're preparing our key move.} 6...O-O 7.h3 {This move stops ...Bg4 and possibly ...Ng4 if Be3. But our thematic strike in the center is ...e5.} 7...e5 8.dxe5 {This will force some exchanges, which is fine. Recapturing with the pawn or the knight (allowing even more trades) is fine.} 8...dxe5 9.Qxd8 {White could decide to remove the queens off the board. Either way, we play chess.} 9...Rxd8 {Black is doing totally fine with the open file, and lack of threats by White. We are also slightly ahead in development.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Nf3 Setups / Study 1: White Plays 3.Nf3 and 4.Bc4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/2f8ce20e-9372-4c12-9f3d-c1d87a62dcf2/studies/a22fcee2-2b53-441d-b87f-502893867fb4/lines"]

1.e4 {Chapter 5 will focus on White developing with Nf3, but not Nc3. This study will focus on the combination between Nf3 and Bc4, and we will use ...e5 as one of the main central counterpushes in this study. White opens with e4 and we will play the Modern.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White places two pawns in the center. We'll fianchetto our bishop.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {White activates the kingside knight 34% of the time in this position, making it the most popular move. With this setup, White usually wants to leave their options open for the c-pawn to move. We'll respond with ...d6 anyway.} 3...d6 4.Bc4 {Nf3-Bc4 is a commonplace setup in chess, and it's played 17% of the time here. We shouldn't play ...Bg4 here because of sacrificial ideas on f7. We're going to continue pressuring the center instead, with ...e5 in the future.} 4...Nc6 5.O-O {White castles, which is completely logical. By playing ...Nf6, we prepare ...Bg4 since the bishop will be protected. We also attack the e4-pawn.} 5...Nf6 6.Re1 {White can defend on e4 using the rook. We'll castle for now. We're preparing our key move.} 6...O-O 7.h3 {This move stops ...Bg4 and possibly ...Ng4 if Be3. But our thematic strike in the center is ...e5.} 7...e5 8.d5 {White may lock things up and attack the knight, which favors us.} 8...Ne7 {We're in great position to play ...Nd7 and ...f5 for instance, and to steamroll White. A move like ...c6 is always useful as well against the structure.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Nf3 Setups / Study 1: White Plays 3.Nf3 and 4.Bc4 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/2f8ce20e-9372-4c12-9f3d-c1d87a62dcf2/studies/a22fcee2-2b53-441d-b87f-502893867fb4/lines"]

1.e4 {Chapter 5 will focus on White developing with Nf3, but not Nc3. This study will focus on the combination between Nf3 and Bc4, and we will use ...e5 as one of the main central counterpushes in this study. White opens with e4 and we will play the Modern.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White places two pawns in the center. We'll fianchetto our bishop.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {White activates the kingside knight 34% of the time in this position, making it the most popular move. With this setup, White usually wants to leave their options open for the c-pawn to move. We'll respond with ...d6 anyway.} 3...d6 4.Bc4 {Nf3-Bc4 is a commonplace setup in chess, and it's played 17% of the time here. We shouldn't play ...Bg4 here because of sacrificial ideas on f7. We're going to continue pressuring the center instead, with ...e5 in the future.} 4...Nc6 5.d5 {Attacking the knight seems very natural.} 5...Nb8 {We don't mind retreating the knight back to its starting square. White has committed to a lot with d5 and now there are some weaknesses in their position. We'll continue with ...Nf6 and ...c6 to undermine the structure.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Nf3 Setups / Study 1: White Plays 3.Nf3 and 4.Bc4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/2f8ce20e-9372-4c12-9f3d-c1d87a62dcf2/studies/a22fcee2-2b53-441d-b87f-502893867fb4/lines"]

1.e4 {Chapter 5 will focus on White developing with Nf3, but not Nc3. This study will focus on the combination between Nf3 and Bc4, and we will use ...e5 as one of the main central counterpushes in this study. White opens with e4 and we will play the Modern.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White places two pawns in the center. We'll fianchetto our bishop.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {White activates the kingside knight 34% of the time in this position, making it the most popular move. With this setup, White usually wants to leave their options open for the c-pawn to move. We'll respond with ...d6 anyway.} 3...d6 4.Bc4 {Nf3-Bc4 is a commonplace setup in chess, and it's played 17% of the time here. We shouldn't play ...Bg4 here because of sacrificial ideas on f7. We're going to continue pressuring the center instead, with ...e5 in the future.} 4...Nc6 5.O-O {White castles, which is completely logical. By playing ...Nf6, we prepare ...Bg4 since the bishop will be protected. We also attack the e4-pawn.} 5...Nf6 6.Qe2 {You might see this move, with the direct idea to defend the e4-pawn, and later try to counteract Black's ...e5 with Rd1. But it allows ...Bg4 with no consequences.} 6...Bg4 7.c3 {White defends d4. But we continue by striking in the center. The move ...e5 is even more potent when the knight on f3 is pinned.} 7...e5 8.Rd1 {Rd1 is a natural way to defend the d4-pawn. We'll castle for now.} 8...O-O 9.h3 {Prompting us to make a decision. We'll take, removing one of the important central defenders. There was no merit to retreating now.} 9...Bxf3 10.Qxf3 {White captures back. Now one of our most important ideas becomes the common ...f5 pawn break. We'll maneuver the knight to d7 first, opening up the pawn and providing more support to e5.} 10...Nd7 11.Be3 {Natural development by White...} 11...Kh8 {And after we move the king, ...f5 becomes a legal move and a crucial idea in our position. If we manage to play it, we will possibly open up the f-file and reach key central control, with attacking potential on the kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Nf3 Setups / Study 1: White Plays 3.Nf3 and 4.Bc4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/2f8ce20e-9372-4c12-9f3d-c1d87a62dcf2/studies/a22fcee2-2b53-441d-b87f-502893867fb4/lines"]

1.e4 {Chapter 5 will focus on White developing with Nf3, but not Nc3. This study will focus on the combination between Nf3 and Bc4, and we will use ...e5 as one of the main central counterpushes in this study. White opens with e4 and we will play the Modern.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White places two pawns in the center. We'll fianchetto our bishop.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {White activates the kingside knight 34% of the time in this position, making it the most popular move. With this setup, White usually wants to leave their options open for the c-pawn to move. We'll respond with ...d6 anyway.} 3...d6 4.Bc4 {Nf3-Bc4 is a commonplace setup in chess, and it's played 17% of the time here. We shouldn't play ...Bg4 here because of sacrificial ideas on f7. We're going to continue pressuring the center instead, with ...e5 in the future.} 4...Nc6 5.O-O {White castles, which is completely logical. By playing ...Nf6, we prepare ...Bg4 since the bishop will be protected. We also attack the e4-pawn.} 5...Nf6 6.Qe2 {You might see this move, with the direct idea to defend the e4-pawn, and later try to counteract Black's ...e5 with Rd1. But it allows ...Bg4 with no consequences.} 6...Bg4 7.c3 {White defends d4. But we continue by striking in the center. The move ...e5 is even more potent when the knight on f3 is pinned.} 7...e5 8.Rd1 {Rd1 is a natural way to defend the d4-pawn. We'll castle for now.} 8...O-O 9.dxe5 {Capturing now runs into the same issues. Just don't play ...dxe5 and hang your queen!} 9...Nxe5 {Same problems for White. They will have to give something up.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Nf3 Setups / Study 1: White Plays 3.Nf3 and 4.Bc4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/2f8ce20e-9372-4c12-9f3d-c1d87a62dcf2/studies/a22fcee2-2b53-441d-b87f-502893867fb4/lines"]

1.e4 {Chapter 5 will focus on White developing with Nf3, but not Nc3. This study will focus on the combination between Nf3 and Bc4, and we will use ...e5 as one of the main central counterpushes in this study. White opens with e4 and we will play the Modern.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White places two pawns in the center. We'll fianchetto our bishop.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {White activates the kingside knight 34% of the time in this position, making it the most popular move. With this setup, White usually wants to leave their options open for the c-pawn to move. We'll respond with ...d6 anyway.} 3...d6 4.Bc4 {Nf3-Bc4 is a commonplace setup in chess, and it's played 17% of the time here. We shouldn't play ...Bg4 here because of sacrificial ideas on f7. We're going to continue pressuring the center instead, with ...e5 in the future.} 4...Nc6 5.O-O {White castles, which is completely logical. By playing ...Nf6, we prepare ...Bg4 since the bishop will be protected. We also attack the e4-pawn.} 5...Nf6 6.Qe2 {You might see this move, with the direct idea to defend the e4-pawn, and later try to counteract Black's ...e5 with Rd1. But it allows ...Bg4 with no consequences.} 6...Bg4 7.c3 {White defends d4. But we continue by striking in the center. The move ...e5 is even more potent when the knight on f3 is pinned.} 7...e5 8.d5 {Once again, we're not really worried about White blocking up the center.} 8...Ne7 {Our counterplay is just heating up! We will go for the standard ...Nd7 and ...f5 idea, since that's our strongest pawn break. We could consider capturing on f3 if we have a reason to.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Nf3 Setups / Study 1: White Plays 3.Nf3 and 4.Bc4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/2f8ce20e-9372-4c12-9f3d-c1d87a62dcf2/studies/a22fcee2-2b53-441d-b87f-502893867fb4/lines"]

1.e4 {Chapter 5 will focus on White developing with Nf3, but not Nc3. This study will focus on the combination between Nf3 and Bc4, and we will use ...e5 as one of the main central counterpushes in this study. White opens with e4 and we will play the Modern.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White places two pawns in the center. We'll fianchetto our bishop.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {White activates the kingside knight 34% of the time in this position, making it the most popular move. With this setup, White usually wants to leave their options open for the c-pawn to move. We'll respond with ...d6 anyway.} 3...d6 4.Bc4 {Nf3-Bc4 is a commonplace setup in chess, and it's played 17% of the time here. We shouldn't play ...Bg4 here because of sacrificial ideas on f7. We're going to continue pressuring the center instead, with ...e5 in the future.} 4...Nc6 5.O-O {White castles, which is completely logical. By playing ...Nf6, we prepare ...Bg4 since the bishop will be protected. We also attack the e4-pawn.} 5...Nf6 6.Qe2 {You might see this move, with the direct idea to defend the e4-pawn, and later try to counteract Black's ...e5 with Rd1. But it allows ...Bg4 with no consequences.} 6...Bg4 7.c3 {White defends d4. But we continue by striking in the center. The move ...e5 is even more potent when the knight on f3 is pinned.} 7...e5 8.dxe5 {This is actually a significant mistake.} 8...Nxe5 {We're going to either shatter White's Kkngside structure, or grab the bishop. Either way, White has allowed us too much activity.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Nf3 Setups / Study 2: Other Nf3 Setups / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/2f8ce20e-9372-4c12-9f3d-c1d87a62dcf2/studies/947363dc-cb9a-4690-bc1d-61ff8f9f0b01/lines"]

1.e4 {In Study 2, we continue analyzing positions after 3.Nf3 by White. This study will focus on fourth move alternatives to 4.Bc4. White begins with e4. We'll reply with the Modern.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White wants the center. We'll fianchetto for now.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {Going for the Nf3 setup. We'll play ...d6.} 3...d6 4.c3 {This is a move you will see 9% of the time, the idea is to defend the center with their pawn. White commits the pawn to the c3-square but wants a solid center. We'll activate the knight, preparing ...e5.} 4...Nc6 5.Bd3 {This is a natural looking way for White to develop. But it allows ...e5, and we will gladly take advantage of that.} 5...e5 6.Be3 {If White waits around, we'll do the same. Developing the knight and eyeing the g4-square.} 6...Nf6 7.h3 {White prevents ...Ng4. We'll just castle.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {Same king safety procedures by White.} 8...d5 {This is a nice move to throw in if the situation presents itself. Our center is well protected, so there's no worry in the world to go into this. If White captures on d5, we can capture on d4. If they capture on e5, we can play ...Nxe4.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Nf3 Setups / Study 2: Other Nf3 Setups / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/2f8ce20e-9372-4c12-9f3d-c1d87a62dcf2/studies/947363dc-cb9a-4690-bc1d-61ff8f9f0b01/lines"]

1.e4 {In Study 2, we continue analyzing positions after 3.Nf3 by White. This study will focus on fourth move alternatives to 4.Bc4. White begins with e4. We'll reply with the Modern.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White wants the center. We'll fianchetto for now.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {Going for the Nf3 setup. We'll play ...d6.} 3...d6 4.c3 {This is a move you will see 9% of the time, the idea is to defend the center with their pawn. White commits the pawn to the c3-square but wants a solid center. We'll activate the knight, preparing ...e5.} 4...Nc6 5.Bb5 {This gives us no worry, although we have to act quickly since White wants d5. We'll attack the bishop.} 5...a6 6.Bxc6+ {On Ba4, we can play ...b5, a move we wanted to play, with tempo. Instead, White captures.} 6...bxc6 {This position is totally fine as we have the bishop pair. We'll try to continue with standard developing moves.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Nf3 Setups / Study 2: Other Nf3 Setups / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/2f8ce20e-9372-4c12-9f3d-c1d87a62dcf2/studies/947363dc-cb9a-4690-bc1d-61ff8f9f0b01/lines"]

1.e4 {In Study 2, we continue analyzing positions after 3.Nf3 by White. This study will focus on fourth move alternatives to 4.Bc4. White begins with e4. We'll reply with the Modern.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White wants the center. We'll fianchetto for now.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {Going for the Nf3 setup. We'll play ...d6.} 3...d6 4.c3 {This is a move you will see 9% of the time, the idea is to defend the center with their pawn. White commits the pawn to the c3-square but wants a solid center. We'll activate the knight, preparing ...e5.} 4...Nc6 5.Bd3 {This is a natural looking way for White to develop. But it allows ...e5, and we will gladly take advantage of that.} 5...e5 6.d5 {As we've seen throughout this course, we're happy if White closes the center. The most logical retreating square is e7. Now ...f5 becomes the main idea.} 6...Nce7 7.c4 {White may try to solidify their center and prepare queenside expansion. But we will play on the kingside!} 7...f5 8.exf5 {You may see this if White panics.} 8...gxf5 {Keeping our pawns in motion. Now ...e4 is coming and White has allowed a lot of central space.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Nf3 Setups / Study 2: Other Nf3 Setups / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/2f8ce20e-9372-4c12-9f3d-c1d87a62dcf2/studies/947363dc-cb9a-4690-bc1d-61ff8f9f0b01/lines"]

1.e4 {In Study 2, we continue analyzing positions after 3.Nf3 by White. This study will focus on fourth move alternatives to 4.Bc4. White begins with e4. We'll reply with the Modern.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White wants the center. We'll fianchetto for now.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {Going for the Nf3 setup. We'll play ...d6.} 3...d6 4.c3 {This is a move you will see 9% of the time, the idea is to defend the center with their pawn. White commits the pawn to the c3-square but wants a solid center. We'll activate the knight, preparing ...e5.} 4...Nc6 5.Bd3 {This is a natural looking way for White to develop. But it allows ...e5, and we will gladly take advantage of that.} 5...e5 6.d5 {As we've seen throughout this course, we're happy if White closes the center. The most logical retreating square is e7. Now ...f5 becomes the main idea.} 6...Nce7 7.c4 {White may try to solidify their center and prepare queenside expansion. But we will play on the kingside!} 7...f5 8.Nc3 {White's best course of action is to develop normally. We'll continue to develop as well. We don't want to take on e4 as that would only help activate White's pieces.} 8...Nf6 9.b4 {White attempts their queenside expansion. We'll try to break up the structure.} 9...a5 10.Qb3 {Logical attempt to protect b4. We'll take. We want to split as many pawns as possible in White's structure.} 10...axb4 11.Qxb4 {White recaptures.} 11...b6 {With the queenside for all intents and purposes locked up, we can begin our kingside assault. Very difficult practically for White to play. They can't really advance on the queenside, while we have a direct approach on the opposite side of the board.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Nf3 Setups / Study 2: Other Nf3 Setups / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/2f8ce20e-9372-4c12-9f3d-c1d87a62dcf2/studies/947363dc-cb9a-4690-bc1d-61ff8f9f0b01/lines"]

1.e4 {In Study 2, we continue analyzing positions after 3.Nf3 by White. This study will focus on fourth move alternatives to 4.Bc4. White begins with e4. We'll reply with the Modern.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White wants the center. We'll fianchetto for now.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {Going for the Nf3 setup. We'll play ...d6.} 3...d6 4.c3 {This is a move you will see 9% of the time, the idea is to defend the center with their pawn. White commits the pawn to the c3-square but wants a solid center. We'll activate the knight, preparing ...e5.} 4...Nc6 5.Bd3 {This is a natural looking way for White to develop. But it allows ...e5, and we will gladly take advantage of that.} 5...e5 6.dxe5 {The exchange is fine... we can take either with the knight or pawn. Just not the bishop, because we don't need to give up the bishop pair. We'll take with the knight here.} 6...Nxe5 7.Nxe5 {White can trade.} 7...dxe5 {And we can play thematic moves from here. We'll get our knight to f6 and potentially h5 if we want to make room for the ...f5 break. We'll castle short, naturally.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Nf3 Setups / Study 2: Other Nf3 Setups / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/2f8ce20e-9372-4c12-9f3d-c1d87a62dcf2/studies/947363dc-cb9a-4690-bc1d-61ff8f9f0b01/lines"]

1.e4 {In Study 2, we continue analyzing positions after 3.Nf3 by White. This study will focus on fourth move alternatives to 4.Bc4. White begins with e4. We'll reply with the Modern.} 1...g6 2.d4 {White wants the center. We'll fianchetto for now.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {Going for the Nf3 setup. We'll play ...d6.} 3...d6 4.Bd3 {Playing Bd3 first runs into ...e5, and it's played 8% of the time. The move ...e5 is a pretty direct antidote to White's approach, as it closes down the bishop permanently and fights for the center.} 4...e5 5.dxe5 {If White takes... By all means, recapture and preserve your bishop.} 5...dxe5 6.O-O {Castling. We'll just develop and support e5.} 6...Nc6 7.Nc3 {White develops too. Prepare to castle kingside.} 7...Nge7 8.h3 {Preventing your bishop from pinning the knight.} 8...O-O {And we castle! White hasn't achieved anything out of the opening and has developed their pieces in a suboptimal way. We'll continue with a comfortable game from here. If allowed, we can go ...Nb4, and if not, ...b6 and ...Bb7 seems fine.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Unique e4 setups / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/2d82d254-1c8d-4bc8-845d-12073623d27b/studies/a2b0d620-23a5-468e-bc17-db7176e2d4f9/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the sixth chapter and our final one on 1. e4 by White. This chapter will focus on Move 2 sidelines by White. As always, White opens with e4. We will go for ...g6.} 1...g6 2.Nc3 {One way that White can develop is Nc3 here, played 8% of the time. White is delaying d4 and wants to protect the pawn first. We will continue with our Modern setup either way} 2...Bg7 3.g3 {White might aspire to do the same. We should control the center with a pawn, since White hasn't contested it in any way. Sure, our bishop will be blocked for now, but so will White's.} 3...e5 4.Bg2 {White develops the bishop to g2. ...Ne7 develops the knight while maintaining flexibility with our f-pawn.} 4...Ne7 5.Nge2 {White does the same. We'll just castle.} 5...O-O 6.O-O {So will White. Once we've reached this position, we've successfully neutralized White's bishop on g2. Now we can begin to focus on the dark squares, and get a strong clamp on the d4-square with ...c5.} 6...c5 7.d3 {White may adopt a similar light-squared strategy. We'll follow through with our dark square dominance.} 7...Nbc6 8.Be3 {White attacks c5. Don't hang it!} 8...d6 {We defend c5, and will continue with our setup. The knight will find itself on d4, and we'll make a light-squared battery with ...Be6 and ...Qd7.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Unique e4 setups / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/2d82d254-1c8d-4bc8-845d-12073623d27b/studies/a2b0d620-23a5-468e-bc17-db7176e2d4f9/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the sixth chapter and our final one on 1. e4 by White. This chapter will focus on Move 2 sidelines by White. As always, White opens with e4. We will go for ...g6.} 1...g6 2.Nc3 {One way that White can develop is Nc3 here, played 8% of the time. White is delaying d4 and wants to protect the pawn first. We will continue with our Modern setup either way} 2...Bg7 3.f4 {This is a reasonable try by White, to control the center in a unique way. They want to preemptively stop our ...e5 ideas. In this case, we will go for ...a6 and ...b5, driven by our previous lessons.} 3...a6 4.a4 {White stops b5, though. So we can transpose into a favorable Sicilian, controlling d4. White is playing a Grand Prix setup now with the pawns on e4 and f4, and we already have the bishop on g7 which is a great antidote on the dark squares.} 4...c5 5.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3. And we'll develop ours behind the pawn.} 5...Nc6 6.Bc4 {White develops the bishop to c4.} 6...e6 {But on c4 it might become a target! We'll play ...e6 to restrict it for now and prepare ...Nge7. At some point, ...d5 is a healthy idea in the center, grabbing some space with tempo on the bishop.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Unique e4 setups / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/2d82d254-1c8d-4bc8-845d-12073623d27b/studies/a2b0d620-23a5-468e-bc17-db7176e2d4f9/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the sixth chapter and our final one on 1. e4 by White. This chapter will focus on Move 2 sidelines by White. As always, White opens with e4. We will go for ...g6.} 1...g6 2.Nc3 {One way that White can develop is Nc3 here, played 8% of the time. White is delaying d4 and wants to protect the pawn first. We will continue with our Modern setup either way} 2...Bg7 3.f4 {This is a reasonable try by White, to control the center in a unique way. They want to preemptively stop our ...e5 ideas. In this case, we will go for ...a6 and ...b5, driven by our previous lessons.} 3...a6 4.a4 {White stops b5, though. So we can transpose into a favorable Sicilian, controlling d4. White is playing a Grand Prix setup now with the pawns on e4 and f4, and we already have the bishop on g7 which is a great antidote on the dark squares.} 4...c5 5.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3. And we'll develop ours behind the pawn.} 5...Nc6 6.g3 {White may rather go this way, so that the bishop is less of a target. No worries, we'll continue placating to our structure.} 6...d6 7.Bg2 {The bishop completes its development.} 7...e6 {But Black has a perfectly playable position. In this case, we'll activate the knight to e7 and castle, remaining flexible. At some point, we can decide for the correct pawn push if needed.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Unique e4 setups / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/2d82d254-1c8d-4bc8-845d-12073623d27b/studies/a2b0d620-23a5-468e-bc17-db7176e2d4f9/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the sixth chapter and our final one on 1. e4 by White. This chapter will focus on Move 2 sidelines by White. As always, White opens with e4. We will go for ...g6.} 1...g6 2.Nc3 {One way that White can develop is Nc3 here, played 8% of the time. White is delaying d4 and wants to protect the pawn first. We will continue with our Modern setup either way} 2...Bg7 3.d4 {This looks familiar! We play our standard move. If White plays any natural move (Nf3, Be3, Bc4, etc.), it will likely transpose to previous parts of the course.} 3...d6 4.g3 {However, this doesn't transpose. White wants to fianchetto the bishop. We'll still aim for our center expansion with ...Nc6 and ...e5. Their bishop on g2 will serve as a strong defender but nothing more.} 4...Nc6 5.Be3 {Natural development by White. We'll follow through, and strike in the center.} 5...e5 6.d5 {White locks it up, which we know by now is good for us! We'll retreat to e7, and ...f5 becomes the priority.} 6...Nce7 7.Bg2 {White finally activates the bishop...} 7...f5 {But we get our structure with ...f5, and can proceed normally from here. We'll activate the kingside knight and castle. We got what we wanted out of the opening.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Unique e4 setups / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/2d82d254-1c8d-4bc8-845d-12073623d27b/studies/a2b0d620-23a5-468e-bc17-db7176e2d4f9/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the sixth chapter and our final one on 1. e4 by White. This chapter will focus on Move 2 sidelines by White. As always, White opens with e4. We will go for ...g6.} 1...g6 2.g3 {You might see people try to mimic your setup, although it happens extremely rarely. Give yourself a pat on the back! We can immediately strike in the center, before White reinforces it.} 2...d5 3.exd5 {Taking is not forced, but logical. We're suddenly exploiting g3, which opens the long diagonal.} 3...Qxd5 4.Nf3 {White blocks with the knight while developing normally. But the knight is pinned multiple ways now!} 4...Bg4 5.Bg2 {Bg2 sufficiently protects the knight. And here's a theoretical move to remember: ...Qe6+! We're either forcing Qe2, after which we trade and the king loses its castling rights, or Kf1, after which the same outcome happens.} 5...Qe6+ 6.Qe2 {Blocking makes the most sense. We should just exchange and take away their castling rights.} 6...Qxe2+ 7.Kxe2 {Black's play is very straightforward from here. We'll develop both knights to the natural squares and fianchetto the dark-squared bishop. Then, we'll castle queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Unique e4 setups / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/2d82d254-1c8d-4bc8-845d-12073623d27b/studies/a2b0d620-23a5-468e-bc17-db7176e2d4f9/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the sixth chapter and our final one on 1. e4 by White. This chapter will focus on Move 2 sidelines by White. As always, White opens with e4. We will go for ...g6.} 1...g6 2.h4 {White plays h4 only 1% of the time, with the idea being to play h5 and break up the kingside structure. You can always play ...h5 yourself, but I have a different recommendation. We strike in the center since White didn't commit there.} 2...d5 3.exd5 {Taking the pawn is very logical. We actually shouldn't recapture with the queen as after Nc3, we get a worse version of the Scandinavian. We'll just develop and eye the pawn instead.} 3...Nf6 4.Nc3 {White develops naturally, giving up the pawn. We don't have to rush with taking it, though. We can just fianchetto.} 4...Bg7 5.h5 {We're not afraid of this move and the following idea by White, but we should mention it. We'll take on h5.} 5...Nxh5 6.Rxh5 {White's idea is very often this exchange sacrifice on h5, but it doesn't work if we play our cards correctly. We'll take, of course.} 6...gxh5 7.Qxh5 {This might be good for White in hyperbullet, but practically, Black is up material, and White does not have many threats.} 7...Nd7 {We can play chess from here, and we're not afraid to play ...Kf8 if we need to defend the bishop. White's only active piece is the queen and they don't have enough attacking prospects to compensate for the sacrifice.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Unique e4 setups / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/2d82d254-1c8d-4bc8-845d-12073623d27b/studies/a2b0d620-23a5-468e-bc17-db7176e2d4f9/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the sixth chapter and our final one on 1. e4 by White. This chapter will focus on Move 2 sidelines by White. As always, White opens with e4. We will go for ...g6.} 1...g6 2.h4 {White plays h4 only 1% of the time, with the idea being to play h5 and break up the kingside structure. You can always play ...h5 yourself, but I have a different recommendation. We strike in the center since White didn't commit there.} 2...d5 3.exd5 {Taking the pawn is very logical. We actually shouldn't recapture with the queen as after Nc3, we get a worse version of the Scandinavian. We'll just develop and eye the pawn instead.} 3...Nf6 4.c4 {White can try to hold on to the pawn like a baby. We don't mind gambiting the pawn.} 4...c6 5.dxc6 {White takes.} 5...Nxc6 {Black will have good piece placement with ...Bg7 and ...Bf5, and be more than able to compensate for the pawn. Plus, now White's pawns on the fourth rank are looking weird.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Unique e4 setups / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/2d82d254-1c8d-4bc8-845d-12073623d27b/studies/a2b0d620-23a5-468e-bc17-db7176e2d4f9/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the sixth chapter and our final one on 1. e4 by White. This chapter will focus on Move 2 sidelines by White. As always, White opens with e4. We will go for ...g6.} 1...g6 2.h4 {White plays h4 only 1% of the time, with the idea being to play h5 and break up the kingside structure. You can always play ...h5 yourself, but I have a different recommendation. We strike in the center since White didn't commit there.} 2...d5 3.h5 {This does not scare us. We'll take a free pawn! White is overestimating the power of their flank push.} 3...dxe4 4.hxg6 {White captures on g6. We'll recapture with the f-pawn not to hang our rook in the corner.} 4...fxg6 5.Nc3 {Going after the pawn. We'll defend.} 5...Nf6 6.Qe2 {Going after the pawn again...} 6...Bf5 {But we protect again! We are simply up a pawn and a central one at that, and will follow up with ...Nc6 and ...Qd7 and often long castle. Eventually, we will play ...Bg7. White hasn't achieved anything.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: White's Triple Center (e4, d4, c4) / Study 1: White Plays 4.Nf3 or 4.Ne2 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/25b02f75-77e5-40fb-a33a-c82ada3311df/studies/13a1ba37-e948-4876-af12-e3dbf3d0342f/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to chapter 7, and the first chapter of our coverage of 1. d4 positions. This chapter will cover White playing d4, e4, and c4. We're going to cover White defending the d4-pawn with their knight in this study (Nf3 or Ne2). White opens with d4, and we go for the Modern with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Playing e4 immediately would transpose to the first half of this course. Black follows through, developing their bishop to g7.} 2...Bg7 3.e4 {You will see this move order 7% of the time. White opts to grab as much central space as humanly possible. While it's good in principle, it has its drawbacks, such as potential overextension. Black targets the center with their knight, coordinating with the bishop on g7. White has to react in order to avoid losing material.} 3...Nc6 4.Nf3 {The most natural defense, played 45% of the time, is developing the knight in the process. We strike in the center, in coordination with our knight and bishop.} 4...e5 5.Be3 {Be3 is another logical attempt by White to deal with e5, this time defending the pawn. We will take on d4, as it'll open up opportunities for us.} 5...exd4 6.Nxd4 {The only recapture that makes sense, as Bxd4 unnecessarily gives up the bishop pair. Instead of trading, we use all three of our pieces honing in on the d4-square to attack the center. Once again, White has to react.} 6...Qf6 7.Nb5 {White may go for a fork on c7. That will not scare us. We take the pawn on b2 with our own attack on a1.} 7...Qxb2 8.Nxc7+ {White has to try Nxc7+. ...Kd8 makes the most sense for the king since it comes with tempo on the knight. Even though it's in the center, White can't really attack it.} 8...Kd8 9.Nxa8 {Taking our rook.} 9...Qxa1 {But we take theirs, and we'll be able to trap the knight in the meantime. Black will emerge up material.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: White's Triple Center (e4, d4, c4) / Study 1: White Plays 4.Nf3 or 4.Ne2 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/25b02f75-77e5-40fb-a33a-c82ada3311df/studies/13a1ba37-e948-4876-af12-e3dbf3d0342f/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to chapter 7, and the first chapter of our coverage of 1. d4 positions. This chapter will cover White playing d4, e4, and c4. We're going to cover White defending the d4-pawn with their knight in this study (Nf3 or Ne2). White opens with d4, and we go for the Modern with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Playing e4 immediately would transpose to the first half of this course. Black follows through, developing their bishop to g7.} 2...Bg7 3.e4 {You will see this move order 7% of the time. White opts to grab as much central space as humanly possible. While it's good in principle, it has its drawbacks, such as potential overextension. Black targets the center with their knight, coordinating with the bishop on g7. White has to react in order to avoid losing material.} 3...Nc6 4.Nf3 {The most natural defense, played 45% of the time, is developing the knight in the process. We strike in the center, in coordination with our knight and bishop.} 4...e5 5.Be3 {Be3 is another logical attempt by White to deal with e5, this time defending the pawn. We will take on d4, as it'll open up opportunities for us.} 5...exd4 6.Nxd4 {The only recapture that makes sense, as Bxd4 unnecessarily gives up the bishop pair. Instead of trading, we use all three of our pieces honing in on the d4-square to attack the center. Once again, White has to react.} 6...Qf6 7.Nxc6 {Capturing the knight is the most logical move by White.} 7...dxc6 {We'll open up the light-squared bishop and our pieces have scope all across the board. The b2-pawn is still vulnerable and we have direct and easy developing plans.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: White's Triple Center (e4, d4, c4) / Study 1: White Plays 4.Nf3 or 4.Ne2 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/25b02f75-77e5-40fb-a33a-c82ada3311df/studies/13a1ba37-e948-4876-af12-e3dbf3d0342f/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to chapter 7, and the first chapter of our coverage of 1. d4 positions. This chapter will cover White playing d4, e4, and c4. We're going to cover White defending the d4-pawn with their knight in this study (Nf3 or Ne2). White opens with d4, and we go for the Modern with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Playing e4 immediately would transpose to the first half of this course. Black follows through, developing their bishop to g7.} 2...Bg7 3.e4 {You will see this move order 7% of the time. White opts to grab as much central space as humanly possible. While it's good in principle, it has its drawbacks, such as potential overextension. Black targets the center with their knight, coordinating with the bishop on g7. White has to react in order to avoid losing material.} 3...Nc6 4.Nf3 {The most natural defense, played 45% of the time, is developing the knight in the process. We strike in the center, in coordination with our knight and bishop.} 4...e5 5.Bg5 {This is the "tricky" move... you probably won't face it a lot, but it is also super logical! White forces Black's hand because we have to play 5... f6, as other ways to block the attack on the queen don't make sense. So we'll kick the bishop away.} 5...f6 6.Be3 {White puts their bishop where they wanted anyway, having forced us to weaken our position with ...f6. In any case, we have to stay consistent with our dark-squared strategy, solidifying the e5-pawn.} 6...d6 7.Nc3 {White develops naturally. How should we continue development now? ...Nh6 looks a bit odd at first glance, but we're going to reroute to f7 and continue our pursue of the dark squares (or ...Ng4 to harass the bishop if they allow it!)} 7...Nh6 8.dxe5 {White always has the realistic possibility of exchanging on e5. And ...fxe5 would sort of ruin our ...f5 strategy. We can actually take with the d-pawn, and accept an early queen trade.} 8...dxe5 9.Qxd8+ {Makes sense for White to accept, as... We can't castle anymore.} 9...Kxd8 10.O-O-O+ {And White castles long with check.} 10...Bd7 {Nevertheless, we have a very solid position. White has no way to attack the bishop on d7 and we'll slide the king to c8 next. Then, we will continue with our plans of rerouting the knight and improving our pieces. At some moment, ...f5 could come.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: White's Triple Center (e4, d4, c4) / Study 1: White Plays 4.Nf3 or 4.Ne2 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/25b02f75-77e5-40fb-a33a-c82ada3311df/studies/13a1ba37-e948-4876-af12-e3dbf3d0342f/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to chapter 7, and the first chapter of our coverage of 1. d4 positions. This chapter will cover White playing d4, e4, and c4. We're going to cover White defending the d4-pawn with their knight in this study (Nf3 or Ne2). White opens with d4, and we go for the Modern with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Playing e4 immediately would transpose to the first half of this course. Black follows through, developing their bishop to g7.} 2...Bg7 3.e4 {You will see this move order 7% of the time. White opts to grab as much central space as humanly possible. While it's good in principle, it has its drawbacks, such as potential overextension. Black targets the center with their knight, coordinating with the bishop on g7. White has to react in order to avoid losing material.} 3...Nc6 4.Nf3 {The most natural defense, played 45% of the time, is developing the knight in the process. We strike in the center, in coordination with our knight and bishop.} 4...e5 5.d5 {This move is by far the most common among amateur players, attempting to dislodge the knight. So we retreat, right? Wrong! We move forward, as our bishop and pawn back it up.} 5...Nd4 6.Be3 {Another logical attempt by White to try and win a pawn outright. An important move, maintaining the tension, and solidifying the knight on our nice d4-post.} 6...c5 7.dxc6 {Though dubious, you likely will face en passant should you allow it.} 7...dxc6 {And our queen contributes to the defense/grip of d4. White has given away their d5-pawn for no reason and now our position is much more open and comfortable. We'll make sure to keep an eye on our knight on d4.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: White's Triple Center (e4, d4, c4) / Study 1: White Plays 4.Nf3 or 4.Ne2 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/25b02f75-77e5-40fb-a33a-c82ada3311df/studies/13a1ba37-e948-4876-af12-e3dbf3d0342f/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to chapter 7, and the first chapter of our coverage of 1. d4 positions. This chapter will cover White playing d4, e4, and c4. We're going to cover White defending the d4-pawn with their knight in this study (Nf3 or Ne2). White opens with d4, and we go for the Modern with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Playing e4 immediately would transpose to the first half of this course. Black follows through, developing their bishop to g7.} 2...Bg7 3.e4 {You will see this move order 7% of the time. White opts to grab as much central space as humanly possible. While it's good in principle, it has its drawbacks, such as potential overextension. Black targets the center with their knight, coordinating with the bishop on g7. White has to react in order to avoid losing material.} 3...Nc6 4.Nf3 {The most natural defense, played 45% of the time, is developing the knight in the process. We strike in the center, in coordination with our knight and bishop.} 4...e5 5.d5 {This move is by far the most common among amateur players, attempting to dislodge the knight. So we retreat, right? Wrong! We move forward, as our bishop and pawn back it up.} 5...Nd4 6.Be3 {Another logical attempt by White to try and win a pawn outright. An important move, maintaining the tension, and solidifying the knight on our nice d4-post.} 6...c5 7.Bd3 {White may attempt to develop normally.} 7...d6 {But once again, our grip on the dark squares gives us good play. Next, we can look to activate our knight to e7 and castle short. After that, ...f5 could be an important pawn break.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: White's Triple Center (e4, d4, c4) / Study 1: White Plays 4.Nf3 or 4.Ne2 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/25b02f75-77e5-40fb-a33a-c82ada3311df/studies/13a1ba37-e948-4876-af12-e3dbf3d0342f/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to chapter 7, and the first chapter of our coverage of 1. d4 positions. This chapter will cover White playing d4, e4, and c4. We're going to cover White defending the d4-pawn with their knight in this study (Nf3 or Ne2). White opens with d4, and we go for the Modern with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Playing e4 immediately would transpose to the first half of this course. Black follows through, developing their bishop to g7.} 2...Bg7 3.e4 {You will see this move order 7% of the time. White opts to grab as much central space as humanly possible. While it's good in principle, it has its drawbacks, such as potential overextension. Black targets the center with their knight, coordinating with the bishop on g7. White has to react in order to avoid losing material.} 3...Nc6 4.Nf3 {The most natural defense, played 45% of the time, is developing the knight in the process. We strike in the center, in coordination with our knight and bishop.} 4...e5 5.dxe5 {Capturing our pawn is always something we need to consider. Only recapture that makes sense, as trading our g7-bishop wouldn't be beneficial.} 5...Nxe5 6.Nxe5 {White doesn't have to take, but likely would, as the bishop on g7 could be a target. Only move!} 6...Bxe5 7.Be2 {White may adopt the "develop and castle" strategy. Solidifying our bishop and the center. If White wants to play in a calm manner, so will we.} 7...d6 8.Qc2 {White could put extra protection on their central pawns.} 8...Ne7 {But once again, Black is doing great on the dark squares. We'll maneuver our knight to c6 from where it can jump to either d4 or b4.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: White's Triple Center (e4, d4, c4) / Study 1: White Plays 4.Nf3 or 4.Ne2 / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/25b02f75-77e5-40fb-a33a-c82ada3311df/studies/13a1ba37-e948-4876-af12-e3dbf3d0342f/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to chapter 7, and the first chapter of our coverage of 1. d4 positions. This chapter will cover White playing d4, e4, and c4. We're going to cover White defending the d4-pawn with their knight in this study (Nf3 or Ne2). White opens with d4, and we go for the Modern with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Playing e4 immediately would transpose to the first half of this course. Black follows through, developing their bishop to g7.} 2...Bg7 3.e4 {You will see this move order 7% of the time. White opts to grab as much central space as humanly possible. While it's good in principle, it has its drawbacks, such as potential overextension. Black targets the center with their knight, coordinating with the bishop on g7. White has to react in order to avoid losing material.} 3...Nc6 4.Ne2 {4. Ne2 is an alternative you probably won't see very often, as it's only played 2% of the time. White might be opting for their own development scheme with g3-Bg2-f4. Once again, we get our way in the center. We don't allow White to have fun!} 4...e5 5.d5 {Once again, d5 is the critical pawn push in these positions. Note that Be3 transposes to our previous line. We refuse to retreat! The knight is backed up by the bishop on g7 and the pawn on e5.} 5...Nd4 6.Nxd4 {White will likely take your knight in practice, and leave you with the doubled d-pawns. We're not worried as our pawn is backed up by the bishop, and our grip on the dark squares is significant.} 6...exd4 7.Bd3 {White may just choose the "develop and castle" route. But ...d6 helps solidify our control of the dark squares, disallowing White any more pawn pushes.} 7...d6 {Black should be in good hands to fight a strategic battle centered on the dark squares. We'll continue with ...Nf6, since our d4-pawn isn't under attack anymore, and castle short. At some point, we'll play ...c5!} 8.O-O {White castles naturally.} 8...Nf6 {Black has a rock-solid position. The d4-pawn is not going anywhere, and will be a thorn. We'll castle next and fight against White's central structure in the long run.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: White's Triple Center (e4, d4, c4) / Study 1: White Plays 4.Nf3 or 4.Ne2 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/25b02f75-77e5-40fb-a33a-c82ada3311df/studies/13a1ba37-e948-4876-af12-e3dbf3d0342f/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to chapter 7, and the first chapter of our coverage of 1. d4 positions. This chapter will cover White playing d4, e4, and c4. We're going to cover White defending the d4-pawn with their knight in this study (Nf3 or Ne2). White opens with d4, and we go for the Modern with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Playing e4 immediately would transpose to the first half of this course. Black follows through, developing their bishop to g7.} 2...Bg7 3.e4 {You will see this move order 7% of the time. White opts to grab as much central space as humanly possible. While it's good in principle, it has its drawbacks, such as potential overextension. Black targets the center with their knight, coordinating with the bishop on g7. White has to react in order to avoid losing material.} 3...Nc6 4.Nf3 {The most natural defense, played 45% of the time, is developing the knight in the process. We strike in the center, in coordination with our knight and bishop.} 4...e5 5.Bg5 {This is the "tricky" move... you probably won't face it a lot, but it is also super logical! White forces Black's hand because we have to play 5... f6, as other ways to block the attack on the queen don't make sense. So we'll kick the bishop away.} 5...f6 6.Be3 {White puts their bishop where they wanted anyway, having forced us to weaken our position with ...f6. In any case, we have to stay consistent with our dark-squared strategy, solidifying the e5-pawn.} 6...d6 7.Nc3 {White develops naturally. How should we continue development now? ...Nh6 looks a bit odd at first glance, but we're going to reroute to f7 and continue our pursue of the dark squares (or ...Ng4 to harass the bishop if they allow it!)} 7...Nh6 8.h3 {Stops ...Ng4.} 8...Nf7 {Even though the g7-bishop is blocked off, Black is doing fine. We'll castle short and look for ...f5 at some moment.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: White's Triple Center (e4, d4, c4) / Study 1: White Plays 4.Nf3 or 4.Ne2 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/25b02f75-77e5-40fb-a33a-c82ada3311df/studies/13a1ba37-e948-4876-af12-e3dbf3d0342f/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to chapter 7, and the first chapter of our coverage of 1. d4 positions. This chapter will cover White playing d4, e4, and c4. We're going to cover White defending the d4-pawn with their knight in this study (Nf3 or Ne2). White opens with d4, and we go for the Modern with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Playing e4 immediately would transpose to the first half of this course. Black follows through, developing their bishop to g7.} 2...Bg7 3.e4 {You will see this move order 7% of the time. White opts to grab as much central space as humanly possible. While it's good in principle, it has its drawbacks, such as potential overextension. Black targets the center with their knight, coordinating with the bishop on g7. White has to react in order to avoid losing material.} 3...Nc6 4.Nf3 {The most natural defense, played 45% of the time, is developing the knight in the process. We strike in the center, in coordination with our knight and bishop.} 4...e5 5.dxe5 {Capturing our pawn is always something we need to consider. Only recapture that makes sense, as trading our g7-bishop wouldn't be beneficial.} 5...Nxe5 6.Nxe5 {White doesn't have to take, but likely would, as the bishop on g7 could be a target. Only move!} 6...Bxe5 7.f4 {We also should be prepared for White to attack the bishop. While this looks good for White, they might be overextending. Retreating our bishop back home.} 7...Bg7 8.Be2 {The idea is to castle next move. But we're going to break up the structure whether White likes it or not!} 8...f5 9.e5 {Black pushes the pawn forward to avoid the tension.} 9...d6 {The position is about to blast open in Black's favor. White has overextended and now their pawn structure on the dark squares is extremely shaky. We'll capitalize on this very soon.} *

