1.d4 {Welcome to chapter 7, and the first chapter of our coverage of 1. d4 positions. This chapter will cover White playing d4, e4, and c4. We're going to cover White defending the d4-pawn with their knight in this study (Nf3 or Ne2). White opens with d4, and we go for the Modern with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Playing e4 immediately would transpose to the first half of this course. Black follows through, developing their bishop to g7.} 2...Bg7 3.e4 {You will see this move order 7% of the time. White opts to grab as much central space as humanly possible. While it's good in principle, it has its drawbacks, such as potential overextension. Black targets the center with their knight, coordinating with the bishop on g7. White has to react in order to avoid losing material.} 3...Nc6 4.Nf3 {The most natural defense, played 45% of the time, is developing the knight in the process. We strike in the center, in coordination with our knight and bishop.} 4...e5 5.d5 {This move is by far the most common among amateur players, attempting to dislodge the knight. So we retreat, right? Wrong! We move forward, as our bishop and pawn back it up.} 5...Nd4 6.Nxd4 {White will likely take your knight in practice, and leave you with the doubled d-pawns. We're not worried as our pawn is backed up by the bishop, and our grip on the dark squares is significant.} 6...exd4 7.Bd3 {White may just choose the "develop and castle" route. But ...d6 helps solidify our control of the dark squares, disallowing White any more pawn pushes.} 7...d6 {Black should be in good hands to fight a strategic battle centered on the dark squares. We'll continue with ...Nf6, since our d4-pawn isn't under attack anymore, and castle short. At some point, we'll play ...c5!} *

[Event "Chapter 7: White's Triple Center (e4, d4, c4) / Study 1: White Plays 4.Nf3 or 4.Ne2 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/25b02f75-77e5-40fb-a33a-c82ada3311df/studies/13a1ba37-e948-4876-af12-e3dbf3d0342f/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to chapter 7, and the first chapter of our coverage of 1. d4 positions. This chapter will cover White playing d4, e4, and c4. We're going to cover White defending the d4-pawn with their knight in this study (Nf3 or Ne2). White opens with d4, and we go for the Modern with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Playing e4 immediately would transpose to the first half of this course. Black follows through, developing their bishop to g7.} 2...Bg7 3.e4 {You will see this move order 7% of the time. White opts to grab as much central space as humanly possible. While it's good in principle, it has its drawbacks, such as potential overextension. Black targets the center with their knight, coordinating with the bishop on g7. White has to react in order to avoid losing material.} 3...Nc6 4.Nf3 {The most natural defense, played 45% of the time, is developing the knight in the process. We strike in the center, in coordination with our knight and bishop.} 4...e5 5.Bg5 {This is the "tricky" move... you probably won't face it a lot, but it is also super logical! White forces Black's hand because we have to play 5... f6, as other ways to block the attack on the queen don't make sense. So we'll kick the bishop away.} 5...f6 6.Bh4 {White may attempt to retreat the bishop while maintaining the pin on the f-pawn. We're going to harass the bishop while putting our kingside pawns in motion.} 6...g5 7.Bg3 {A sacrifice on g5 makes no sense. Bg3 is forced.} 7...h5 {The bishop as well as the kingside is a target now. White has allowed us a lot of tempi and will have to spend some moves untangling themselves from this position now.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: White's Triple Center (e4, d4, c4) / Study 2: White Plays 4.Be3 or 4.d5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/25b02f75-77e5-40fb-a33a-c82ada3311df/studies/7633f5e5-1f91-40bf-b844-8f62cc83757c/lines"]

1.d4 {This study will focus on other moves White can play to defend the d4-pawn. White begins with d4. Let's go for ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {White plays c4. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.e4 {White wants the setup with three pawns in the center. We'll activate our knight to target one of them.} 3...Nc6 4.Be3 {White might choose to defend the pawn with Be3, and they do so 20% of the time. We'll pressure the center with our e-pawn.} 4...e5 5.d5 {White's most natural response is to push the d-pawn and attack the knight. Once again, the knight, supported by the bishop and the pawn, moves forward!} 5...Nd4 6.f4 {White may attempt to soften up the defense of the knight. We can't take on f4, so we will provide additional support to the knight once again. Now taking on f4 is viable.} 6...c5 7.fxe5 {Taking the pawn is not practically great, but you may see this. White doesn't want to allow us to capture on f4. But this allows ...Qh4+! White shouldn't block because of the hanging e4-pawn.} 7...Qh4+ 8.Kd2 {White's best attempt is to move the king.} 8...Bh6 {And there's an even stronger move than capturing on e4 now. There's no checkmate imminent, but White will be at a disadvantage due to the placement of their king. This is not how White should play.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: White's Triple Center (e4, d4, c4) / Study 2: White Plays 4.Be3 or 4.d5 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/25b02f75-77e5-40fb-a33a-c82ada3311df/studies/7633f5e5-1f91-40bf-b844-8f62cc83757c/lines"]

1.d4 {This study will focus on other moves White can play to defend the d4-pawn. White begins with d4. Let's go for ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {White plays c4. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.e4 {White wants the setup with three pawns in the center. We'll activate our knight to target one of them.} 3...Nc6 4.d5 {Attacking the knight immediately with the pawn is an important consideration. It's played 19% of the time. We'll respond to White's aggression by jumping our knight to the center.} 4...Nd4 5.Ne2 {White now develops a piece while threatening the knight. Taking on e2 wouldn't make sense, as it would help White's development after Bxe2 and O-O. So we'll support the knight instead.} 5...c5 6.Nxd4 {Capturing the knight makes the most sense, as to continue with the development process with Be2 or Bd3. The pawn on d4 is now well protected. ...Bxd4 would have possibly left the bishop as a target sitting in the center.} 6...cxd4 7.Nd2 {This maneuver might be critical, as Nb3 plans to corral the pawn, and it's not easy to defend. We no longer have a c-pawn, and ...e5 doesn't work because our bishop will be blocked out. We're not going to worry at the moment, though. ...Qb6 will sufficiently defend the d-pawn.} 7...d6 8.Nb3 {Attacking our pawn. But we defend it with the queen.} 8...Qb6 9.Bd2 {One sneaky idea White may try. The threat is Ba5, which nearly traps the Queen.} 9...a5 {Which is why this move is important to remember. If either piece takes on a5, we're happy to receive the two minors for the rook. If not, we'll look to push ...a4 on the queenside and finish kingside development with ...Nf6, ...Nd7 and castle.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: White's Triple Center (e4, d4, c4) / Study 2: White Plays 4.Be3 or 4.d5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/25b02f75-77e5-40fb-a33a-c82ada3311df/studies/7633f5e5-1f91-40bf-b844-8f62cc83757c/lines"]

1.d4 {This study will focus on other moves White can play to defend the d4-pawn. White begins with d4. Let's go for ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {White plays c4. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.e4 {White wants the setup with three pawns in the center. We'll activate our knight to target one of them.} 3...Nc6 4.Be3 {White might choose to defend the pawn with Be3, and they do so 20% of the time. We'll pressure the center with our e-pawn.} 4...e5 5.d5 {White's most natural response is to push the d-pawn and attack the knight. Once again, the knight, supported by the bishop and the pawn, moves forward!} 5...Nd4 6.Nc3 {White's smartest choice might be to develop without harassing the knight, at least for the time being. We'll give our knight more support. We're playing same correct setup in almost every line in this study.} 6...c5 7.dxc6 {En passant opens up a critical piece for Black.} 7...dxc6 {Now the queen contributes to the protection of d4. We already know by now that this is good for us.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: White's Triple Center (e4, d4, c4) / Study 2: White Plays 4.Be3 or 4.d5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/25b02f75-77e5-40fb-a33a-c82ada3311df/studies/7633f5e5-1f91-40bf-b844-8f62cc83757c/lines"]

1.d4 {This study will focus on other moves White can play to defend the d4-pawn. White begins with d4. Let's go for ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {White plays c4. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.e4 {White wants the setup with three pawns in the center. We'll activate our knight to target one of them.} 3...Nc6 4.Be3 {White might choose to defend the pawn with Be3, and they do so 20% of the time. We'll pressure the center with our e-pawn.} 4...e5 5.d5 {White's most natural response is to push the d-pawn and attack the knight. Once again, the knight, supported by the bishop and the pawn, moves forward!} 5...Nd4 6.Ne2 {Ne2 right away puts more pressure on the knight on d4. We support the knight in our usual fashion.} 6...c5 7.dxc6 {If en passant...} 7...dxc6 {We recapture away from the center, and our queen joins in the defense. Good luck for White in getting rid of our knight on d4!} *

[Event "Chapter 7: White's Triple Center (e4, d4, c4) / Study 2: White Plays 4.Be3 or 4.d5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/25b02f75-77e5-40fb-a33a-c82ada3311df/studies/7633f5e5-1f91-40bf-b844-8f62cc83757c/lines"]

1.d4 {This study will focus on other moves White can play to defend the d4-pawn. White begins with d4. Let's go for ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {White plays c4. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.e4 {White wants the setup with three pawns in the center. We'll activate our knight to target one of them.} 3...Nc6 4.d5 {Attacking the knight immediately with the pawn is an important consideration. It's played 19% of the time. We'll respond to White's aggression by jumping our knight to the center.} 4...Nd4 5.Nc3 {White develops the knight to a natural square. We're going to preemptively defend our knight.} 5...c5 6.Be3 {White isn't quite threatening material, as the knight is protected multiple times. However... We should still back up the Knight!} 6...e5 7.dxe6 {We know by now... just because en passant is legal does not make it brilliant. We'll recapture with the d-pawn, opening up the queen.} 7...dxe6 8.Nf3 {How can we protect the d4-knight even more?} 8...Ne7 {With the other knight! Our immediate idea is to play ...Nec6, solidify the knight on d4, and continue playing chess from there. We'll castle short.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: White's Triple Center (e4, d4, c4) / Study 2: White Plays 4.Be3 or 4.d5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/25b02f75-77e5-40fb-a33a-c82ada3311df/studies/7633f5e5-1f91-40bf-b844-8f62cc83757c/lines"]

1.d4 {This study will focus on other moves White can play to defend the d4-pawn. White begins with d4. Let's go for ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {White plays c4. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.e4 {White wants the setup with three pawns in the center. We'll activate our knight to target one of them.} 3...Nc6 4.Be3 {White might choose to defend the pawn with Be3, and they do so 20% of the time. We'll pressure the center with our e-pawn.} 4...e5 5.d5 {White's most natural response is to push the d-pawn and attack the knight. Once again, the knight, supported by the bishop and the pawn, moves forward!} 5...Nd4 6.Nc3 {White's smartest choice might be to develop without harassing the knight, at least for the time being. We'll give our knight more support. We're playing same correct setup in almost every line in this study.} 6...c5 7.Bd3 {Once again, White takes the "develop and castle" route, without immediate regard to the knight on d4. That gives us time to solidify the center completely.} 7...d6 8.Nge2 {White finally pressures the d4-knight, on the basis that they get to castle in the near future. We can ignore the attack for now and complete kingside development, as our knight is sufficiently defended. We'll bring the knight to h6, eyeing the g4-square.} 8...Nh6 9.f3 {White prevents ...Ng4. However, the knight from h6 protects another square. Our thematic pawn break! Possibly with ideas of ...f4, ...g5 and ...g4 and a kingside pawn storm.} 9...f5 10.exf5 {White takes the pawn, as otherwise ...f4 is an idea for Black.} 10...Nhxf5 {Black's knights prove to be a nuisance, and Black should be doing just fine. We are attacking the bishop on e3 and the knights protect each other nicely. If White retreats to f2, we can consider bringing our queen to g5 and castling.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: White's Triple Center (e4, d4, c4) / Study 2: White Plays 4.Be3 or 4.d5 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/25b02f75-77e5-40fb-a33a-c82ada3311df/studies/7633f5e5-1f91-40bf-b844-8f62cc83757c/lines"]

1.d4 {This study will focus on other moves White can play to defend the d4-pawn. White begins with d4. Let's go for ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {White plays c4. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.e4 {White wants the setup with three pawns in the center. We'll activate our knight to target one of them.} 3...Nc6 4.d5 {Attacking the knight immediately with the pawn is an important consideration. It's played 19% of the time. We'll respond to White's aggression by jumping our knight to the center.} 4...Nd4 5.Be3 {Another way White may pressure our knight. Our strategy should not change! We'll continue on the dark squares with ...c5.} 5...c5 6.dxc6 {As we know by now, en passant does not scare us. The queen is also open now.} 6...dxc6 7.Nc3 {White develops a piece. Our knight is being treated like a king... Lots of body guards! All jokes aside, it's paramount to keep the knight on d4 and overprotect it, as it serves the most important purpose in our position.} 7...e5 8.Nge2 {White puts more "pressure" on the knight, but it does not really accomplish much.} 8...f5 {Once we've solidified the knight, we'll begin our standard kingside strategy! If allowed, we'll even push ...f4. Understanding the weaknesses in White's setup and playing against them is how we got a positional edge out of the opening. Although the engine thinks the advantage is small, I'd much rather have Black here.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: White's Triple Center (e4, d4, c4) / Study 2: White Plays 4.Be3 or 4.d5 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/25b02f75-77e5-40fb-a33a-c82ada3311df/studies/7633f5e5-1f91-40bf-b844-8f62cc83757c/lines"]

1.d4 {This study will focus on other moves White can play to defend the d4-pawn. White begins with d4. Let's go for ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {White plays c4. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.e4 {White wants the setup with three pawns in the center. We'll activate our knight to target one of them.} 3...Nc6 4.d5 {Attacking the knight immediately with the pawn is an important consideration. It's played 19% of the time. We'll respond to White's aggression by jumping our knight to the center.} 4...Nd4 5.Ne2 {White now develops a piece while threatening the knight. Taking on e2 wouldn't make sense, as it would help White's development after Bxe2 and O-O. So we'll support the knight instead.} 5...c5 6.Nxd4 {Capturing the knight makes the most sense, as to continue with the development process with Be2 or Bd3. The pawn on d4 is now well protected. ...Bxd4 would have possibly left the bishop as a target sitting in the center.} 6...cxd4 7.Bd3 {Preparing castling. White doesn't want to chase after our d4-pawn yet. Let's grab some space and open up some pieces.} 7...d6 8.O-O {Castling!} 8...Nf6 {We'll castle, and use the dark squares to our advantage next. Our knight will maneuver to d7 and from there to either c5 or e5. Once again, we are utilizing the strong aspects of our position and setup.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: White's Triple Center (e4, d4, c4) / Study 2: White Plays 4.Be3 or 4.d5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/25b02f75-77e5-40fb-a33a-c82ada3311df/studies/7633f5e5-1f91-40bf-b844-8f62cc83757c/lines"]

1.d4 {This study will focus on other moves White can play to defend the d4-pawn. White begins with d4. Let's go for ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {White plays c4. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.e4 {White wants the setup with three pawns in the center. We'll activate our knight to target one of them.} 3...Nc6 4.Be3 {White might choose to defend the pawn with Be3, and they do so 20% of the time. We'll pressure the center with our e-pawn.} 4...e5 5.dxe5 {Once again, capturing the pawn is a critical component to these positions. We don't need to recapture the pawn. The move ...d6 helps open our bishop and gives us more options. Sometimes gambits like these come in handy.} 5...d6 6.exd6 {White may actually do this, recognizing that ...Bxb2 and ...Bxa1 is extremely risky. We'll go ahead and take the pawn.} 6...Bxb2 7.Nd2 {Now the queen is protecting the rook.} 7...Qxd6 {But our idea wasn't to capture it as that would leave a big hole on the dark squares, which is a risk we don't want to take. We'll just recapture the pawn on d6 instead. Next, we'll activate our kingside knight and castle short.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Accelerated King's Indian / Study 1: White Plays 5.d5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/bd8cb81d-6d27-4aea-90ff-74d3bae9d829/studies/cc08fd9c-2917-4aa6-8961-77d5b167010c/lines"]

1.d4 {This chapter will focus on the more traditional response to d4, c4 and Nc3. It's less aggressive and exciting than the Beefeater, but as we've seen throughout the course, this structure is extremely viable. White begins with d4. We'll play ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Black follows through, developing their bishop to g7.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {This is a standard approach by White. Instead of going for the early ...c5, this chapter will focus on a more solid alternative. We'll play ...d6 here, and essentially play a KID structure.} 3...d6 4.e4 {Grabbing the entire center is by far White's most natural move. We've essentially transposed into a KID, but haven't committed with ...Nf6 yet. This pawn break is very thematic in the KID, attempting to pressure the center immediately, and put White's pawn center into question.} 4...e5 5.d5 {Played 58% of the time, d5 is the most common and natural response for White, to gain space and maintain their center control. In a perfect world, White would play b4, c5, and storm the queenside. So, ...a5 temporarily stops b4.} 5...a5 6.Bd3 {White develops their bishop naturally. We're in position to plant the knight on the c5-square, eyeing the bishop and controlling the center.} 6...Na6 7.h4 {Going h4-h5 is something we always need to consider for White in these kingside fianchetto structures. We can stop h5 by playing ...h5 ourselves.} 7...h5 8.Bg5 {Bg5 is now a serious possibility, attacking our queen. Moving the queen would leave our pieces stuck together. So we'll kick the bishop with ...f6.} 8...f6 9.Bd2 {White retreats.} 9...Bh6 {The bishop exchange favors us as our bishop was stuck behind our pawns. And it's hard for White to avoid this trade now. After that, we'll continue development.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Accelerated King's Indian / Study 1: White Plays 5.d5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/bd8cb81d-6d27-4aea-90ff-74d3bae9d829/studies/cc08fd9c-2917-4aa6-8961-77d5b167010c/lines"]

1.d4 {This chapter will focus on the more traditional response to d4, c4 and Nc3. It's less aggressive and exciting than the Beefeater, but as we've seen throughout the course, this structure is extremely viable. White begins with d4. We'll play ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Black follows through, developing their bishop to g7.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {This is a standard approach by White. Instead of going for the early ...c5, this chapter will focus on a more solid alternative. We'll play ...d6 here, and essentially play a KID structure.} 3...d6 4.e4 {Grabbing the entire center is by far White's most natural move. We've essentially transposed into a KID, but haven't committed with ...Nf6 yet. This pawn break is very thematic in the KID, attempting to pressure the center immediately, and put White's pawn center into question.} 4...e5 5.d5 {Played 58% of the time, d5 is the most common and natural response for White, to gain space and maintain their center control. In a perfect world, White would play b4, c5, and storm the queenside. So, ...a5 temporarily stops b4.} 5...a5 6.Bd3 {White develops their bishop naturally. We're in position to plant the knight on the c5-square, eyeing the bishop and controlling the center.} 6...Na6 7.a3 {The move a3 serves the purpose of stopping ...Nb4, as it could be annoying, but also to prepare b4 in the future. We hit the bishop and plant our knight on the c5-square.} 7...Nc5 8.Bc2 {White doesn't want to concede the bishop pair, so Bc2 is pretty thematic here. Our knight will go to e7, where we keep the option of ...f5 open.} 8...Ne7 9.Rb1 {It's unlikely that you'll see this exact plan by White, but it's still good to see common ideas in these openings. Rb1 has the idea to play b4 while sufficiently protected. We're now going to strike with ...f5, with possible ideas of taking on e4 or playing ...f4 against a castled king and launching a kingside attack.} 9...f5 10.b4 {White attacks on the queenside themselves. We'll take.} 10...axb4 11.axb4 {White captures back. We should take the pawn on e4, as the alternatives are passive.} 11...Nxe4 12.Nxe4 {White makes the trade.} 12...fxe4 {We have a very solid position to work with. We have the open a-file for the rook and the f5-square for either our knight or our bishop. Once we castle short, our f-rook will have the semi-open file as well.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Accelerated King's Indian / Study 1: White Plays 5.d5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/bd8cb81d-6d27-4aea-90ff-74d3bae9d829/studies/cc08fd9c-2917-4aa6-8961-77d5b167010c/lines"]

1.d4 {This chapter will focus on the more traditional response to d4, c4 and Nc3. It's less aggressive and exciting than the Beefeater, but as we've seen throughout the course, this structure is extremely viable. White begins with d4. We'll play ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {White puts a second pawn in the center. Black follows through, developing their bishop to g7.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {This is a standard approach by White. Instead of going for the early ...c5, this chapter will focus on a more solid alternative. We'll play ...d6 here, and essentially play a KID structure.} 3...d6 4.e4 {Grabbing the entire center is by far White's most natural move. We've essentially transposed into a KID, but haven't committed with ...Nf6 yet. This pawn break is very thematic in the KID, attempting to pressure the center immediately, and put White's pawn center into question.} 4...e5 5.d5 {Played 58% of the time, d5 is the most common and natural response for White, to gain space and maintain their center control. In a perfect world, White would play b4, c5, and storm the queenside. So, ...a5 temporarily stops b4.} 5...a5 6.Nge2 {White may chose this development scheme. They want the knight on g3. We play ...h5, with the idea to meet Ng3 with ...h4.} 6...h5 7.Be3 {White develops normally. The bishop exchange favors us, as White has a great bishop, and our bishop is not so great.} 7...Bh6 8.Bxh6 {White takes.} 8...Nxh6 {Black has a solid position. We'll continue on the queenside with moves like ...b6 to connect the pawns, and maneuvering our knight to c5 via a6.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Accelerated King's Indian / Study 2: White Plays 5.dxe5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/bd8cb81d-6d27-4aea-90ff-74d3bae9d829/studies/05e75e7d-132f-4813-a354-58492188d032/lines"]

1.d4 {Next, we will cover the ensuing positions where White exchanges on e5, and trades queens. Queen trades may not be desirable for a lot of you, so it's important to understand how to navigate the resulting positions. White opens with d4, and we'll continue with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {White goes for Nc3. Let's keep it solid with ...d6.} 3...d6 4.e4 {White wants the whole center. We'll strike back with ...e5 right away.} 4...e5 5.dxe5 {Trading is not objectively best, as it relieves the tension early. It's played 13% of the time. But it is something we want to consider, as White has the option to exchange queens next move. We'll take back with the pawn to keep the central presence.} 5...dxe5 6.Qxd8+ {White exchanges queens. We recapture, losing our castling rights. That's alright as White isn't really playing for an advantage making these trades.} 6...Kxd8 7.f4 {f4 is a common move in this position. White wants to retake the center and activate their pieces. We'll calmly defend the center.} 7...Nc6 8.Nf3 {White applies more pressure in the center. Since it's defended, we'll develop our bishop. Nothing out of the ordinary.} 8...Be6 9.Be3 {White does the same. And we'll complete our development. A viable idea is ...Ng4, especially if White captures on e5.} 9...Nf6 10.h3 {Which is what White prevents.} 10...Kc8 {Sidestepping any d-file pressure with ...Kc8.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Accelerated King's Indian / Study 2: White Plays 5.dxe5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/bd8cb81d-6d27-4aea-90ff-74d3bae9d829/studies/05e75e7d-132f-4813-a354-58492188d032/lines"]

1.d4 {Next, we will cover the ensuing positions where White exchanges on e5, and trades queens. Queen trades may not be desirable for a lot of you, so it's important to understand how to navigate the resulting positions. White opens with d4, and we'll continue with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {White goes for Nc3. Let's keep it solid with ...d6.} 3...d6 4.e4 {White wants the whole center. We'll strike back with ...e5 right away.} 4...e5 5.dxe5 {Trading is not objectively best, as it relieves the tension early. It's played 13% of the time. But it is something we want to consider, as White has the option to exchange queens next move. We'll take back with the pawn to keep the central presence.} 5...dxe5 6.Qxd8+ {White exchanges queens. We recapture, losing our castling rights. That's alright as White isn't really playing for an advantage making these trades.} 6...Kxd8 7.Bg5+ {This check does not bother us the least bit. We'll block with the pawn and hit the bishop.} 7...f6 8.O-O-O+ {White castles long with check. After trading queens, this is usually the way they want to grab tempi. We're not worried.} 8...Nd7 9.Be3 {White retreats the bishop. And we'll implement the same bishop exchange strategy.} 9...Bh6 10.Bxh6 {White takes. We'll capture back.} 10...Nxh6 11.f3 {White may go f3 to solidify e4.} 11...c6 {But Black still stands solid here with multiple possible plans. We have covered some key central squares and will go ...Kc7 to unpin our knight. Then, we will improve our pieces on the queenside. On the kingside, we can consider ...f5.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Accelerated King's Indian / Study 2: White Plays 5.dxe5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/bd8cb81d-6d27-4aea-90ff-74d3bae9d829/studies/05e75e7d-132f-4813-a354-58492188d032/lines"]

1.d4 {Next, we will cover the ensuing positions where White exchanges on e5, and trades queens. Queen trades may not be desirable for a lot of you, so it's important to understand how to navigate the resulting positions. White opens with d4, and we'll continue with ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {White goes for Nc3. Let's keep it solid with ...d6.} 3...d6 4.e4 {White wants the whole center. We'll strike back with ...e5 right away.} 4...e5 5.dxe5 {Trading is not objectively best, as it relieves the tension early. It's played 13% of the time. But it is something we want to consider, as White has the option to exchange queens next move. We'll take back with the pawn to keep the central presence.} 5...dxe5 6.Qxd8+ {White exchanges queens. We recapture, losing our castling rights. That's alright as White isn't really playing for an advantage making these trades.} 6...Kxd8 7.Be3 {White develops while preparing to castle long and use the rook for an attack. We'll also develop normally. We get our bishop out before blocking the check with the knight.} 7...Be6 8.O-O-O+ {White castles with check. And we'll block with the knight without a problem.} 8...Nd7 9.Nf3 {White develops, maybe hoping to harass our Bishop. That's why ...f6 prevents the knight's advancement. Our bishop is blocked in for the moment, but we have a plan for it.} 9...f6 10.Be2 {White develops and connects the rooks. A trade we've seen before, which favors us.} 10...Bh6 11.Bxh6 {White obliges. We'll recapture and activate our knight.} 11...Nxh6 12.Rd2 {White prepares to double the rooks on the d-file.} 12...c6 {But ...c6 will negate any forward knight movement, and we have a very solid position to play. We can unpin with ...Ke7 and play for ...f5 to bring life to our position. Once our rooks are connected, we can consider bringing them to the center.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Accelerated King's Indian / Study 3: White Plays 5.Nf3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/bd8cb81d-6d27-4aea-90ff-74d3bae9d829/studies/20de7ade-f9f7-4120-918e-fdca65423d4b/lines"]

1.d4 {In the third study, we're going to be analyzing probably one of the more conventional approaches for White, simply developing and defending the pawn on d4 with Nf3. Our developing setups will change accordingly. White begins with d4. We will play ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. Let's fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight. We'll continue with ...d6.} 3...d6 4.e4 {White places another pawn on e4. We'll strike with ...e5.} 4...e5 5.Nf3 {This is a very conventional approach to Black's ...e5 push, and it's played 15% of the time. Just protecting the pawn and continuing development. So, ...Nc6 will be our developing choice to continue targeting the d4-pawn, and provoke d5.} 5...Nc6 6.d5 {White does push d5, but commits to a locked up structure, which we don't mind at all! We'll simply retreat to e7, and you guessed it. Our main idea becomes ...f5 to target White's structure!} 6...Nce7 7.Be2 {White develops and prepares to castle. But we're prepared to attack on the kingside, so we strike with ...f5!} 7...f5 8.O-O {White could ignore our pawn thrust and simply castle. In that case, we'll continue improving. We don't want to commit to capturing on e4 yet, if ever.} 8...Nf6 9.Nd2 {White rotates the knight to d2.} 9...f4 {Instead of capturing, I recommend pushing ...f4 here. Locking up the center and grabbing kingside space is extremely potent, especially since White has moved one defender away from the kingside. We'll continue with ...g5 and try to attack the king.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Accelerated King's Indian / Study 3: White Plays 5.Nf3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/bd8cb81d-6d27-4aea-90ff-74d3bae9d829/studies/20de7ade-f9f7-4120-918e-fdca65423d4b/lines"]

1.d4 {In the third study, we're going to be analyzing probably one of the more conventional approaches for White, simply developing and defending the pawn on d4 with Nf3. Our developing setups will change accordingly. White begins with d4. We will play ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. Let's fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight. We'll continue with ...d6.} 3...d6 4.e4 {White places another pawn on e4. We'll strike with ...e5.} 4...e5 5.Nf3 {This is a very conventional approach to Black's ...e5 push, and it's played 15% of the time. Just protecting the pawn and continuing development. So, ...Nc6 will be our developing choice to continue targeting the d4-pawn, and provoke d5.} 5...Nc6 6.dxe5 {The exchange does not scare us. In this case, we'll recapture with the knight. We don't want a queen trade.} 6...Nxe5 7.Nxe5 {White captures.} 7...Bxe5 {Once again, keeping the pieces on the board. Black has a solid position. There are multiple approaches we can choose here. It's only important we finish developing our pieces and castle.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The Accelerated King's Indian / Study 3: White Plays 5.Nf3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/bd8cb81d-6d27-4aea-90ff-74d3bae9d829/studies/20de7ade-f9f7-4120-918e-fdca65423d4b/lines"]

1.d4 {In the third study, we're going to be analyzing probably one of the more conventional approaches for White, simply developing and defending the pawn on d4 with Nf3. Our developing setups will change accordingly. White begins with d4. We will play ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. Let's fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 {White activates the knight. We'll continue with ...d6.} 3...d6 4.e4 {White places another pawn on e4. We'll strike with ...e5.} 4...e5 5.Nf3 {This is a very conventional approach to Black's ...e5 push, and it's played 15% of the time. Just protecting the pawn and continuing development. So, ...Nc6 will be our developing choice to continue targeting the d4-pawn, and provoke d5.} 5...Nc6 6.Be3 {Continuing to protect d4 is also an option by White. We're going to pin the knight which is protecting the center, trying to restrict White's defenders.} 6...Bg4 7.d5 {Taking on e5 is likely not wise as ...Nxe5 gives Black an active position. Instead, White pushes d5. We're not worried. We'll put our front foot forward with our knight!} 7...Nd4 8.Be2 {Breaking the pin. We exchange on f3 to ensure that the knight on d4 has as few challengers as possible.} 8...Bxf3 9.Bxf3 {White recaptures.} 9...c5 {Our knight is solidified after ...c5, and we're not worried about en passant. If White captures on d4, we'll capture back and have a nice protected passed pawn. Apart from that, we'll simply finish kingside development next and castle.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: White Plays d4, c4, Nf3 / Study 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/d319cafe-5b90-4326-8420-584a88adf687/studies/bdfa2bf4-2f00-491f-87a2-dfd112c05ea9/lines"]

1.d4 {In Chapter 9, we will be covering White developing with Nf3 after d4/c4 rather than Nc3. A lot of the ideas are the same, but there could be some nuances. White begins with d4. Let's reply with the usual ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll go for the fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {Nf3 indicates flexible development for White, and it's played 18% of the time. They could still go for Nc3 and e4, or g3 and Bg2 setups. We'll play ...d6 as we normally do, preparing ...Nd7 and/or ...e5 in the future.} 3...d6 4.Nc3 {This study will focus on Nc3, followed by e3 or d5. Nc3 is still played almost 60% of the time here. White is playing the Two Knights setup and wants a lot of central control. We'll go ...Nc6 and begin to pressure the center, too, as we have to contest it somehow.} 4...Nc6 5.d5 {Attacking the knight straight away is something we have to consider, although it's played only 7% of the time. ...Ne5 is the most active move, as it's backed up by the bishop.} 5...Ne5 6.Nxe5 {White takes. We'll recapture with the bishop to keep it active.} 6...Bxe5 7.e4 {This is White's best move, taking as much center space as possible. We'll now threaten to take on c3 followed by capturing on e4!} 7...Nf6 8.Bd3 {White activates the light-squared bishop and prepares castling. We'll castle.} 8...O-O 9.O-O {So will White. Once we've secured our king, it's time to think about the center. The best way to challenge White's structure is ...e6. We might want to launch a kingside attack in the future.} 9...e6 10.dxe6 {Let's analyze an example line if White takes. We'll recapture with the bishop to activate it. Things could go wrong quickly for White here.} 10...Bxe6 11.f4 {For example, f4 tries to force things. We can give a check on d4.} 11...Bd4+ 12.Kh1 {White moves the king. But we suddenly have an attack flying in. We'll bring the knight to g4, with the idea of sliding the queen to h4! We're also threatening to jump to f2.} 12...Ng4 13.Qe2 {White's last chance to keep the position in the balance was Qe1 or f5. However, Qe2 loses to a tactic. We can capture on h2!} 13...Nxh2 14.Kxh2 {White shouldn't take, of course, but if they do...} 14...Qh4# {Checkmate!} *

[Event "Chapter 9: White Plays d4, c4, Nf3 / Study 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/d319cafe-5b90-4326-8420-584a88adf687/studies/bdfa2bf4-2f00-491f-87a2-dfd112c05ea9/lines"]

1.d4 {In Chapter 9, we will be covering White developing with Nf3 after d4/c4 rather than Nc3. A lot of the ideas are the same, but there could be some nuances. White begins with d4. Let's reply with the usual ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll go for the fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {Nf3 indicates flexible development for White, and it's played 18% of the time. They could still go for Nc3 and e4, or g3 and Bg2 setups. We'll play ...d6 as we normally do, preparing ...Nd7 and/or ...e5 in the future.} 3...d6 4.Nc3 {This study will focus on Nc3, followed by e3 or d5. Nc3 is still played almost 60% of the time here. White is playing the Two Knights setup and wants a lot of central control. We'll go ...Nc6 and begin to pressure the center, too, as we have to contest it somehow.} 4...Nc6 5.e3 {This is a little timid by White, however, it's also non-committal. It's played 18% of the time. White blocks their dark-squared bishop but keeps the center strong. We'll simply continue developing and prepare to castle.} 5...Nf6 6.Be2 {White will do the same. Let's castle.} 6...O-O 7.d5 {It's important to analyze White delaying d5, so that now if ...Ne5 we're no longer protected by the bishop. Believe it or not, we're well positioned to play ...Nb8! We can always reroute to d7 later, and we still have the same central/kingside expansions available as well.} 7...Nb8 8.O-O {White castles. But we're prepared to strike in the center.} 8...e5 9.e4 {White doesn't want to give us too much space. In that case, we have an alternative plan. We want our knight on f4 and to play ...f5 in the near future, potentially starting an attack on the kingside.} 9...Nh5 10.Ne1 {White drops back to e1. Let's jump to our desired square. White taking on f4 would only give us a pawn deep in their position and activate our bishop.} 10...Nf4 11.Nd3 {White offers a trade instead. But we'll take the bishop on e2 instead, with check.} 11...Nxe2+ 12.Qxe2 {White recaptures.} 12...f5 {And now we can go for our ...f5 idea. If allowed, we'll play ...f4 and ...g5 and try to attack. We also have the bishop pair now and White's knight on d3 isn't doing much.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: White Plays d4, c4, Nf3 / Study 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/d319cafe-5b90-4326-8420-584a88adf687/studies/bdfa2bf4-2f00-491f-87a2-dfd112c05ea9/lines"]

1.d4 {In Chapter 9, we will be covering White developing with Nf3 after d4/c4 rather than Nc3. A lot of the ideas are the same, but there could be some nuances. White begins with d4. Let's reply with the usual ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll go for the fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {Nf3 indicates flexible development for White, and it's played 18% of the time. They could still go for Nc3 and e4, or g3 and Bg2 setups. We'll play ...d6 as we normally do, preparing ...Nd7 and/or ...e5 in the future.} 3...d6 4.Nc3 {This study will focus on Nc3, followed by e3 or d5. Nc3 is still played almost 60% of the time here. White is playing the Two Knights setup and wants a lot of central control. We'll go ...Nc6 and begin to pressure the center, too, as we have to contest it somehow.} 4...Nc6 5.e3 {This is a little timid by White, however, it's also non-committal. It's played 18% of the time. White blocks their dark-squared bishop but keeps the center strong. We'll simply continue developing and prepare to castle.} 5...Nf6 6.Be2 {White will do the same. Let's castle.} 6...O-O 7.d5 {It's important to analyze White delaying d5, so that now if ...Ne5 we're no longer protected by the bishop. Believe it or not, we're well positioned to play ...Nb8! We can always reroute to d7 later, and we still have the same central/kingside expansions available as well.} 7...Nb8 8.O-O {White castles. But we're prepared to strike in the center.} 8...e5 9.dxe6 {White can also take en passant. That only activates our bishop.} 9...Bxe6 10.Nd4 {White goes after the Bishop} 10...Bd7 {We'll just retreat it and keep a solid position. We should play ...Nc6 next again to offer a trade of knights, since there is no pawn on d5 anymore.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: White Plays d4, c4, Nf3 / Study 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/d319cafe-5b90-4326-8420-584a88adf687/studies/bdfa2bf4-2f00-491f-87a2-dfd112c05ea9/lines"]

1.d4 {In Chapter 9, we will be covering White developing with Nf3 after d4/c4 rather than Nc3. A lot of the ideas are the same, but there could be some nuances. White begins with d4. Let's reply with the usual ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll go for the fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {Nf3 indicates flexible development for White, and it's played 18% of the time. They could still go for Nc3 and e4, or g3 and Bg2 setups. We'll play ...d6 as we normally do, preparing ...Nd7 and/or ...e5 in the future.} 3...d6 4.Nc3 {This study will focus on Nc3, followed by e3 or d5. Nc3 is still played almost 60% of the time here. White is playing the Two Knights setup and wants a lot of central control. We'll go ...Nc6 and begin to pressure the center, too, as we have to contest it somehow.} 4...Nc6 5.e3 {This is a little timid by White, however, it's also non-committal. It's played 18% of the time. White blocks their dark-squared bishop but keeps the center strong. We'll simply continue developing and prepare to castle.} 5...Nf6 6.Be2 {White will do the same. Let's castle.} 6...O-O 7.O-O {White may castle as well. That gives us some space and activity in the center with ...e5. Since White hasn't challenged us in any way, we'll strike first!} 7...e5 8.d5 {White pushes forward, attacking our knight.} 8...Ne7 {We retreat, and we're prepared to launch a kingside attack in the near future. We'll retreat our knight to e7 and play for ...f5. Familiar ideas.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: White Plays d4, c4, Nf3 / Study 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/d319cafe-5b90-4326-8420-584a88adf687/studies/bdfa2bf4-2f00-491f-87a2-dfd112c05ea9/lines"]

1.d4 {In Chapter 9, we will be covering White developing with Nf3 after d4/c4 rather than Nc3. A lot of the ideas are the same, but there could be some nuances. White begins with d4. Let's reply with the usual ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll go for the fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {Nf3 indicates flexible development for White, and it's played 18% of the time. They could still go for Nc3 and e4, or g3 and Bg2 setups. We'll play ...d6 as we normally do, preparing ...Nd7 and/or ...e5 in the future.} 3...d6 4.Nc3 {This study will focus on Nc3, followed by e3 or d5. Nc3 is still played almost 60% of the time here. White is playing the Two Knights setup and wants a lot of central control. We'll go ...Nc6 and begin to pressure the center, too, as we have to contest it somehow.} 4...Nc6 5.d5 {Attacking the knight straight away is something we have to consider, although it's played only 7% of the time. ...Ne5 is the most active move, as it's backed up by the bishop.} 5...Ne5 6.Nxe5 {White takes. We'll recapture with the bishop to keep it active.} 6...Bxe5 7.f4 {White may try to attack the bishop early. We'll take rather than retreat here. We want to disrupt White's pawn structure, and it's okay to give up our bishop.} 7...Bxc3+ 8.bxc3 {White recaptures.} 8...Nf6 {...Nf6 prevents e4, and we have a very comfortable position. We'll continue with ...Bf5 and castle. In the long run, we do have to worry about our dark squares since we traded off our bishop, but some of White's pawns are weak as well.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: White Plays d4, c4, Nf3 / Study 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/d319cafe-5b90-4326-8420-584a88adf687/studies/bdfa2bf4-2f00-491f-87a2-dfd112c05ea9/lines"]

1.d4 {In Chapter 9, we will be covering White developing with Nf3 after d4/c4 rather than Nc3. A lot of the ideas are the same, but there could be some nuances. White begins with d4. Let's reply with the usual ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll go for the fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {Nf3 indicates flexible development for White, and it's played 18% of the time. They could still go for Nc3 and e4, or g3 and Bg2 setups. We'll play ...d6 as we normally do, preparing ...Nd7 and/or ...e5 in the future.} 3...d6 4.Nc3 {This study will focus on Nc3, followed by e3 or d5. Nc3 is still played almost 60% of the time here. White is playing the Two Knights setup and wants a lot of central control. We'll go ...Nc6 and begin to pressure the center, too, as we have to contest it somehow.} 4...Nc6 5.d5 {Attacking the knight straight away is something we have to consider, although it's played only 7% of the time. ...Ne5 is the most active move, as it's backed up by the bishop.} 5...Ne5 6.Nxe5 {White takes. We'll recapture with the bishop to keep it active.} 6...Bxe5 7.e4 {This is White's best move, taking as much center space as possible. We'll now threaten to take on c3 followed by capturing on e4!} 7...Nf6 8.f4 {White might forget about our threats and instinctively attack the bishop. We'll take.} 8...Bxc3+ 9.bxc3 {White recaptures. But their e4-pawn is hanging.} 9...Nxe4 10.Qd4 {White can try this fork, but it's hardly anything to worry about.} 10...Nf6 {We can simply retreat and block the attack on the rook. We are a clean pawn up. This is not how White should play.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: White Plays d4, c4, Nf3 / Study 2: White Plays 4.e4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/d319cafe-5b90-4326-8420-584a88adf687/studies/380e5dca-1e91-443b-b793-abec05c6779d/lines"]

1.d4 {This study will focus on White playing an early e4, as opposed to e3 where they block the bishop or d5 where they commit to a structure early. White opens with the Queen's Pawn. We'll respond with the Modern.} 1...g6 2.c4 {Grabbing more central space. We'll place our bishop on g7.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {White plays the flexible Nf3. We'll go for ...d6.} 3...d6 4.e4 {Instead of playing with both knights, White might choose to place all three pawns in the center here. This occurs 8% of the time. I recommend pinning the knight first, so that we can play ...Nc6 later and put pressure on d4.} 4...Bg4 5.Be2 {Be2 unpins the knight and prepares castling. Instead of capturing right away, we'll play ...Nc6 first to attack the d4-pawn. This may prompt White to push d5.} 5...Nc6 6.Be3 {White defends the d4-pawn instead. We've placed all of our pieces on the optimal squares. Now all that's left is to strike in the center!} 6...e5 7.dxe5 {White could take on e5, but it does not worry us in the least bit. We'll capture back with the pawn, maintaining control of the center. We are okay with trading queens if White wants to.} 7...dxe5 8.O-O {White castles instead. We can continue with our development without any issues.} 8...Nf6 9.Nc3 {Suppose White develops their knight. We'll castle.} 9...O-O {We'll castle.} 10.Qxd8 {And if the queens are traded...} 10...Rfxd8 {We just recapture and we'll be perfectly fine in the resulting position. There's no opening advantage for White whatsoever. They can't even jump to the d5-square as the e4-pawn would be hanging.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: White Plays d4, c4, Nf3 / Study 2: White Plays 4.e4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/d319cafe-5b90-4326-8420-584a88adf687/studies/380e5dca-1e91-443b-b793-abec05c6779d/lines"]

1.d4 {This study will focus on White playing an early e4, as opposed to e3 where they block the bishop or d5 where they commit to a structure early. White opens with the Queen's Pawn. We'll respond with the Modern.} 1...g6 2.c4 {Grabbing more central space. We'll place our bishop on g7.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {White plays the flexible Nf3. We'll go for ...d6.} 3...d6 4.e4 {Instead of playing with both knights, White might choose to place all three pawns in the center here. This occurs 8% of the time. I recommend pinning the knight first, so that we can play ...Nc6 later and put pressure on d4.} 4...Bg4 5.Be2 {Be2 unpins the knight and prepares castling. Instead of capturing right away, we'll play ...Nc6 first to attack the d4-pawn. This may prompt White to push d5.} 5...Nc6 6.d5 {White could try to attack our knight here. We'll throw in the capture on f3. We're paving the way for the knight to jump to d4.} 6...Bxf3 7.Bxf3 {White recaptures. And now we can place our knight in the center. We'll solidify it even more soon.} 7...Nd4 8.O-O {White castles.} 8...c5 {And ...c5 strengthens the knight. We're not afraid of en passant as it removes White's central pawn and we can simply recapture. We'll complete kingside development next and castle. We don't necessarily want to capture on f3.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: White Plays d4, c4, Nf3 / Study 2: White Plays 4.e4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/d319cafe-5b90-4326-8420-584a88adf687/studies/380e5dca-1e91-443b-b793-abec05c6779d/lines"]

1.d4 {This study will focus on White playing an early e4, as opposed to e3 where they block the bishop or d5 where they commit to a structure early. White opens with the Queen's Pawn. We'll respond with the Modern.} 1...g6 2.c4 {Grabbing more central space. We'll place our bishop on g7.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {White plays the flexible Nf3. We'll go for ...d6.} 3...d6 4.e4 {Instead of playing with both knights, White might choose to place all three pawns in the center here. This occurs 8% of the time. I recommend pinning the knight first, so that we can play ...Nc6 later and put pressure on d4.} 4...Bg4 5.Be2 {Be2 unpins the knight and prepares castling. Instead of capturing right away, we'll play ...Nc6 first to attack the d4-pawn. This may prompt White to push d5.} 5...Nc6 6.Be3 {White defends the d4-pawn instead. We've placed all of our pieces on the optimal squares. Now all that's left is to strike in the center!} 6...e5 7.d5 {Of course, White could choose to push d5 here and attack our knight. Same idea... paving the way for Nd4!} 7...Bxf3 8.Bxf3 {White recaptures. We'll activate our knight on d4, where it's already protected.} 8...Nd4 9.Bxd4 {White may try to take, claiming that we have a "weak" pawn on d4. The thing is, it's not weak at all! Our bishop nicely safeguards it, and there's no great way for White to attack it.} 9...exd4 10.Nd2 {White continues developing. They might want to maneuver the knight to b3 at some point. We'll prepare castling and develop to e7 because we want to keep protecting the d4-pawn.} 10...Ne7 11.O-O {White castles.} 11...O-O {So will we. If White ever jumps to b3, attacking our d4-pawn, we can play ...c5! In that case, en passant would be met with ...Nxc6. We won't be losing our d4-pawn yet. It serves as a thorn in White's position.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: White Plays d4, c4, Nf3 / Study 2: White Plays 4.e4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/d319cafe-5b90-4326-8420-584a88adf687/studies/380e5dca-1e91-443b-b793-abec05c6779d/lines"]

1.d4 {This study will focus on White playing an early e4, as opposed to e3 where they block the bishop or d5 where they commit to a structure early. White opens with the Queen's Pawn. We'll respond with the Modern.} 1...g6 2.c4 {Grabbing more central space. We'll place our bishop on g7.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {White plays the flexible Nf3. We'll go for ...d6.} 3...d6 4.e4 {Instead of playing with both knights, White might choose to place all three pawns in the center here. This occurs 8% of the time. I recommend pinning the knight first, so that we can play ...Nc6 later and put pressure on d4.} 4...Bg4 5.Be3 {White may choose to overprotect the d4-pawn, anticipating our ideas. We'll still take, removing one of the defenders.} 5...Bxf3 6.Qxf3 {White recaptures with the queen. This move is the most popular, but it's actually inferior to gxf3. Because we have ...c5, challenging the center and indirectly putting pressure on the b2-pawn!} 6...c5 7.Qd1 {White could go back with the queen, supporting the pawn.} 7...Nc6 {But after we pile up even more pressure on the d4-pawn, White won't be able to hold the position together. Black is already much better. If White wants to play all three pawns in the center, they have to know how to keep it stable!} *

[Event "Chapter 9: White Plays d4, c4, Nf3 / Study 3: White Plays 4.e3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/d319cafe-5b90-4326-8420-584a88adf687/studies/cc891c9c-619d-4dfd-8c6e-9fd78dfd2c3a/lines"]

1.d4 {This study will focus on an interesting development scheme by White, developing with Nf3, e3, Nc3 and Be2. Let's go. White opens with d4. We'll play the Modern.} 1...g6 2.c4 {Two active pawns. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {Activating the knight to f3. Let's go for ...d6.} 3...d6 4.e3 {This approach is rather tame, and Black can actually get a nice development scheme out of this. White solidifes the center but blocks their bishop 11% of the time. We're going to start with ...Nd7, and play directly for ...e5 in the center.} 4...Nd7 5.Nc3 {White develops the knight to a natural square. We'll go ahead and launch our pawn. Not only does this improve our central control, but when we stick a pawn on e4, it'll help assist a kingside attack.} 5...e5 6.d5 {This is terrible for White positionally. We can continue expanding with ...f5. It creates threats of taking over the center and the kingside.} 6...f5 7.e4 {That's why White may actually try to play e4 for themselves. But now I suggest we develop. We are fine with White capturing on f5.} 7...Ngf6 8.exf5 {And they do. We recapture, of course. And we have two very dangerous pawns. The g-file opening is not dangerous for us, as White has more important things to worry about.} 8...gxf5 9.Be2 {Developing is quite natural. Let's start focusing on improvement now. The knight is much stronger on c5.} 9...Nc5 10.Ng5 {White plays Ng5, threatening to slide the bishop to h5 with check. But we can let them do so! We'll continue playing on the queenside and prevent any b4 ideas by White.} 10...a5 11.Bh5+ {White gives the check. As long as we don't make the mistake of capturing the bishop and activating White's queen, we'll be fine. Let's slide to f8.} 11...Kf8 12.Nf7 {And if White forks you...} 12...Qe8 {The idea is to pin the knight to the bishop. This will essentially earn us two pieces for the rook. White has overestimated their potential and now Black is much better.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: White Plays d4, c4, Nf3 / Study 3: White Plays 4.e3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/d319cafe-5b90-4326-8420-584a88adf687/studies/cc891c9c-619d-4dfd-8c6e-9fd78dfd2c3a/lines"]

1.d4 {This study will focus on an interesting development scheme by White, developing with Nf3, e3, Nc3 and Be2. Let's go. White opens with d4. We'll play the Modern.} 1...g6 2.c4 {Two active pawns. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {Activating the knight to f3. Let's go for ...d6.} 3...d6 4.e3 {This approach is rather tame, and Black can actually get a nice development scheme out of this. White solidifes the center but blocks their bishop 11% of the time. We're going to start with ...Nd7, and play directly for ...e5 in the center.} 4...Nd7 5.Nc3 {White develops the knight to a natural square. We'll go ahead and launch our pawn. Not only does this improve our central control, but when we stick a pawn on e4, it'll help assist a kingside attack.} 5...e5 6.dxe5 {White could choose to exchange, but that hardly works in their favor. We'll recapture with the pawn.} 6...dxe5 7.Be2 {White develops. And we develop too.} 7...Ngf6 8.O-O {White castles. And so do we.} 8...O-O 9.e4 {Play could continue with e4. A logical move to give the dark-squared bishop life. Although White can't jump to d5 immediately, we should still cover that square for the future.} 9...c6 10.Bg5 {This is a very natural move, pinning our knight and developing the bishop. But it doesn't really threaten much.} 10...Nc5 {But we can propose a queen trade, while putting serious pressure on White's e-pawn. Although we're not dealing with super sharp and theoretical lines, the positions aren't trivial, and both sides have to be careful! If White takes on e5, we'll trade queens and take on e4 with an advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: White Plays d4, c4, Nf3 / Study 3: White Plays 4.e3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/d319cafe-5b90-4326-8420-584a88adf687/studies/cc891c9c-619d-4dfd-8c6e-9fd78dfd2c3a/lines"]

1.d4 {This study will focus on an interesting development scheme by White, developing with Nf3, e3, Nc3 and Be2. Let's go. White opens with d4. We'll play the Modern.} 1...g6 2.c4 {Two active pawns. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {Activating the knight to f3. Let's go for ...d6.} 3...d6 4.e3 {This approach is rather tame, and Black can actually get a nice development scheme out of this. White solidifes the center but blocks their bishop 11% of the time. We're going to start with ...Nd7, and play directly for ...e5 in the center.} 4...Nd7 5.Nc3 {White develops the knight to a natural square. We'll go ahead and launch our pawn. Not only does this improve our central control, but when we stick a pawn on e4, it'll help assist a kingside attack.} 5...e5 6.Be2 {White is generally better off maintaining the tension. We'll do the same. No need to commit to ...exd4 or ...e4 before developing and castling.} 6...Ngf6 7.O-O {White castles. We'll do the same.} 7...O-O 8.Qc2 {White might aim for the queenside here, bringing the queen to c2 and preparing a4 and b4. In this case, we want to answer with central aggression and push ...e4, so we'll prepare it. After locking up the center, we want to be quicker on the kingside than White on the queenside.} 8...Re8 9.b4 {White begins their queenside launch. We'll push in the center as a response, attacking the knight.} 9...e4 10.Nd2 {White retreats, attacking the e4-pawn.} 10...Qe7 {But our attacking scheme is very straightforward. We'll go for ...h5 and try to maneuver our knight to g5 via f8 and h7. Other ideas include ...Ng4 and ...Qh4.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: Fianchetto Setups / Study 1: The Catalan / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/77bdcaf9-593a-4b83-9797-498413570a76/studies/17c34456-8678-43e9-a0cc-f5c97c305753/lines"]

1.d4 {This study will explore positions that arise after White chooses to go for the famous Catalan Setup. In this setup, White fianchettoes on the kingside, usually after playing the pawns to d4 and c4. We'll be able to implement our Modern motifs here too! White opens with d4, and we'll play ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {White takes a different approach than examined in the last chapter. White is showing less commitment by developing their knight to f3. We'll play ...d6 to open the square for our knight.} 3...d6 4.g3 {You might see this idea by White, to play g3-Bg2 and control the long diagonal. We'll continue to pressure the center.} 4...Nc6 5.d5 {This looks good for White, but the truth is that it's a little bit flimsy. The pawns do grab central space, but also leave some dark squares open. ...Ne5 is great here to play in the center.} 5...Ne5 6.Nxe5 {White takes. We capture back with the bishop.} 6...Bxe5 7.f4 {White tries to gain more space with tempo. We'll just retreat, and the more pawns White pushes the more unstable and extended their structure becomes. Moreover, they are taking away some squares from their own pieces.} 7...Bg7 8.e4 {White continues to push even more. This might look good on the surface, since White is controlling a big portion of the board.} 8...e6 {But in reality, we can easily chip away at the central structure, and Black has a very healthy position compared to White's. Our bishop is standing strong on g7, and we'll continue to fight for the center with ...c6, and place our queen on the active b6-square at some point.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: Fianchetto Setups / Study 1: The Catalan / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/77bdcaf9-593a-4b83-9797-498413570a76/studies/17c34456-8678-43e9-a0cc-f5c97c305753/lines"]

1.d4 {This study will explore positions that arise after White chooses to go for the famous Catalan Setup. In this setup, White fianchettoes on the kingside, usually after playing the pawns to d4 and c4. We'll be able to implement our Modern motifs here too! White opens with d4, and we'll play ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {White takes a different approach than examined in the last chapter. White is showing less commitment by developing their knight to f3. We'll play ...d6 to open the square for our knight.} 3...d6 4.g3 {You might see this idea by White, to play g3-Bg2 and control the long diagonal. We'll continue to pressure the center.} 4...Nc6 5.Bg2 {White places their bishop on g2. And we'll go ...Bd7! The devious idea is to go ...Qc8 and ...Bh3 and try to attack the kingside. There's not a whole lot White can do against our plan.} 5...Bd7 6.O-O {White can castle... But that means they are essentially allowing ...Bh3, because now they can't play h3.} 6...Qc8 7.Re1 {Here's an example line where we get ...Bh3 in. Attempting to trade the bishop, and weaken White's King. This is great because we sometimes have trouble activating our light-squared bishop anyway in these kinds of positions.} 7...Bh3 8.Bh1 {White drops back, which is the purpose behind Re1. We will not give up our pursuit on the kingside. Let's try to push and attack!} 8...h5 9.Nc3 {White will play Nc3 at some point. But ...h4 is coming whether White likes it or not. Not much can be done about it. Black is ready to rip the king apart.} 9...h4 10.Nxh4 {It may seem like White can capture with the knight, but that's a game-ending blunder. We'll sacrifice, of course!} 10...Rxh4 11.gxh4 {White shouldn't capture but if they do...} 11...Qg4+ {There's checkmate in two. White has no moves but to block, after which we will capture on g2. Good game!} *

[Event "Chapter 10: Fianchetto Setups / Study 1: The Catalan / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/77bdcaf9-593a-4b83-9797-498413570a76/studies/17c34456-8678-43e9-a0cc-f5c97c305753/lines"]

1.d4 {This study will explore positions that arise after White chooses to go for the famous Catalan Setup. In this setup, White fianchettoes on the kingside, usually after playing the pawns to d4 and c4. We'll be able to implement our Modern motifs here too! White opens with d4, and we'll play ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {White takes a different approach than examined in the last chapter. White is showing less commitment by developing their knight to f3. We'll play ...d6 to open the square for our knight.} 3...d6 4.g3 {You might see this idea by White, to play g3-Bg2 and control the long diagonal. We'll continue to pressure the center.} 4...Nc6 5.Bg2 {White places their bishop on g2. And we'll go ...Bd7! The devious idea is to go ...Qc8 and ...Bh3 and try to attack the kingside. There's not a whole lot White can do against our plan.} 5...Bd7 6.O-O {White can castle... But that means they are essentially allowing ...Bh3, because now they can't play h3.} 6...Qc8 7.Re1 {Here's an example line where we get ...Bh3 in. Attempting to trade the bishop, and weaken White's King. This is great because we sometimes have trouble activating our light-squared bishop anyway in these kinds of positions.} 7...Bh3 8.Bh1 {White drops back, which is the purpose behind Re1. We will not give up our pursuit on the kingside. Let's try to push and attack!} 8...h5 9.Nc3 {White will play Nc3 at some point. But ...h4 is coming whether White likes it or not. Not much can be done about it. Black is ready to rip the king apart.} 9...h4 10.d5 {White can try counterattacking in the center. That's usually a good antidote against a flank attack, but here White might be too late. We'll capture on g3 and sacrifice our knight!} 10...hxg3 11.dxc6 {White can try taking, but we have a devastating move.} 11...g2 {Now the bishop is gone. Apart from that, White's shelter is completely exposed and we will easily be able to attack. If anything, White capturing on b7 would give us the diagonal as well. Black is much better, if not winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: Fianchetto Setups / Study 1: The Catalan / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/77bdcaf9-593a-4b83-9797-498413570a76/studies/17c34456-8678-43e9-a0cc-f5c97c305753/lines"]

1.d4 {This study will explore positions that arise after White chooses to go for the famous Catalan Setup. In this setup, White fianchettoes on the kingside, usually after playing the pawns to d4 and c4. We'll be able to implement our Modern motifs here too! White opens with d4, and we'll play ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {White takes a different approach than examined in the last chapter. White is showing less commitment by developing their knight to f3. We'll play ...d6 to open the square for our knight.} 3...d6 4.g3 {You might see this idea by White, to play g3-Bg2 and control the long diagonal. We'll continue to pressure the center.} 4...Nc6 5.Bg2 {White places their bishop on g2. And we'll go ...Bd7! The devious idea is to go ...Qc8 and ...Bh3 and try to attack the kingside. There's not a whole lot White can do against our plan.} 5...Bd7 6.O-O {White can castle... But that means they are essentially allowing ...Bh3, because now they can't play h3.} 6...Qc8 7.Re1 {Here's an example line where we get ...Bh3 in. Attempting to trade the bishop, and weaken White's King. This is great because we sometimes have trouble activating our light-squared bishop anyway in these kinds of positions.} 7...Bh3 8.Bh1 {White drops back, which is the purpose behind Re1. We will not give up our pursuit on the kingside. Let's try to push and attack!} 8...h5 9.Nc3 {White will play Nc3 at some point. But ...h4 is coming whether White likes it or not. Not much can be done about it. Black is ready to rip the king apart.} 9...h4 10.d5 {White can try counterattacking in the center. That's usually a good antidote against a flank attack, but here White might be too late. We'll capture on g3 and sacrifice our knight!} 10...hxg3 11.fxg3 {White should recapture on g3.} 11...Ne5 {We continue to play against White's weak King. Trading off one more defender would be great, which is why we played ...Ne5. We have the h-file and will just continue to improve from here.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: Fianchetto Setups / Study 1: The Catalan / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/77bdcaf9-593a-4b83-9797-498413570a76/studies/17c34456-8678-43e9-a0cc-f5c97c305753/lines"]

1.d4 {This study will explore positions that arise after White chooses to go for the famous Catalan Setup. In this setup, White fianchettoes on the kingside, usually after playing the pawns to d4 and c4. We'll be able to implement our Modern motifs here too! White opens with d4, and we'll play ...g6.} 1...g6 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.Nf3 {White takes a different approach than examined in the last chapter. White is showing less commitment by developing their knight to f3. We'll play ...d6 to open the square for our knight.} 3...d6 4.g3 {You might see this idea by White, to play g3-Bg2 and control the long diagonal. We'll continue to pressure the center.} 4...Nc6 5.Bg2 {White places their bishop on g2. And we'll go ...Bd7! The devious idea is to go ...Qc8 and ...Bh3 and try to attack the kingside. There's not a whole lot White can do against our plan.} 5...Bd7 6.Nc3 {White develops naturally. We follow through with ...Qc8.} 6...Qc8 7.h3 {White can try to defend Bh3 by playing h3. However, now White shouldn't castle, because it would drop a pawn!} 7...Nf6 {So Black can play with the advantage of White not being able to easily castle. We'll castle ourselves and play ...a6 to take away the b5-square. If White goes for e4 here, we should play ...e5.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: Other 1st Moves / Study 1: Basically Any Other White 1st Move / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/3c0940d2-f2b7-4f2c-869f-c40d913a5178/studies/6e5681e6-8b70-4ab5-a817-4ab378ad3369/lines"]

1.Nf3 1...g6 2.g3 {White employs what's called the "King's Indian Attack", preparing Bg2. It's like the KID, but on the White side! We'll fianchetto, naturally.} 2...Bg7 3.Bg2 {White continues their similar setup to ours. Honestly, if White goes this long without claiming the center, we should go ahead and do it ourselves.} 3...e5 4.d3 {The most common continuation of White's KIA setup. White wants to play e4. We have two pawns in the center and our Modern setup!} 4...d5 5.O-O {White castles. We'll nurture our center.} 5...Nc6 6.Nbd2 {White prepares e4, which is their main idea. We'll develop our knight to e7, keeping ...f5 as an option, which is our main idea!} 6...Nge7 7.e4 {White strikes in the center. But we have enough defenders on the d5-pawn.} 7...O-O 8.c3 {White goes c3, possibly preparing a b4-advance.} 8...a5 {But ...a5 slows down White's queenside play.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: Other 1st Moves / Study 1: Basically Any Other White 1st Move / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/3c0940d2-f2b7-4f2c-869f-c40d913a5178/studies/6e5681e6-8b70-4ab5-a817-4ab378ad3369/lines"]

1.Nf3 1...g6 2.g3 {White employs what's called the "King's Indian Attack", preparing Bg2. It's like the KID, but on the White side! We'll fianchetto, naturally.} 2...Bg7 3.Bg2 {White continues their similar setup to ours. Honestly, if White goes this long without claiming the center, we should go ahead and do it ourselves.} 3...e5 4.O-O {White could also castle here and go for a different setup, aiming for d4. This gives Black a lot of space and isn't played often at the highest level, but we should learn how to face it anyway. Grabbing central space with the two pawns is always good.} 4...d5 5.d4 {White could try and challenge the center with d4... But ...e4 simply grabs more space and attacks the knight at the same time.} 5...e4 6.Ng5 {White shoves the knight forward, but without any targets for the moment. Their main idea is to put c4 into motion. Simply developing, ready to castle.} 6...Nf6 7.c4 {Challenging the center. We want to kick the knight out first, before dealing with the c-pawn.} 7...h6 8.Nh3 {White is forced to retreat. Capturing the knight with ...Bxh3 is a bad trade for us, as their knight isn't doing much! And now simply capturing on c4 keeps the position comfortable for Black. Playing ...c6 is also possible, but I recommend leaving the c6-square for the knight to develop to.} 8...dxc4 9.Na3 {White might go straight for the c4-pawn. But we can let them take it!} 9...O-O 10.Nxc4 {White spent a lot of moves developing, challenging the center and getting the pawn back. We still have a lot of room to develop and expand, though. I like the simple ...Nc6 here, attacking the d4-pawn.} 10...Nc6 11.e3 {White is basically forced to play e3 and block their own dark-squared bishop!} 11...g5 {The last move I want you to remember is ...g5. Having already played ...h6, this move completely restricts White's knight from getting back into the game. Although it does weaken the kingside a bit, we have a dark-squared bishop that's a great defender!} *

[Event "Chapter 11: Other 1st Moves / Study 1: Basically Any Other White 1st Move / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/3c0940d2-f2b7-4f2c-869f-c40d913a5178/studies/6e5681e6-8b70-4ab5-a817-4ab378ad3369/lines"]

1.d4 {1.d4 is technically not a move 1 deviation, but there could be move 2 deviations. We will play 1... g6 as we normally do.} 1...g6 2.Bf4 {Of course, the London setups could be something you face even against 1...g6. We'll continue developing for now.} 2...Bg7 3.e3 {White continue their London approach. We'll play ...d6, though, and the position is much different than the traditional London where our pawn is on d5. White's bishop isn't as strong.} 3...d6 4.Nf3 {White develops the knight. We're going to prepare ...e5, which assaults the "London Bishop". In many of these first move alternatives, changing our approach to the quick ...e5 is the way to go.} 4...Nd7 5.h3 {White gives the bishop a safe haven. We'll attack it anyway.} 5...e5 6.Bh2 {White retreats it. We're going to develop our knight to h6 to keep the bishop open and prepare the possibility of ...f5. As you already know, this pawn push is extremely helpful in some Modern positions.} 6...Nh6 7.Be2 {White develops and prepares to castle. We'll castle ourselves.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White castles as well. But we have a dynamic position to counter the London.} 8...f5 9.c4 {White takes some more central space on the other side. But maybe they misunderstood our intentions after ...f5.} 9...g5 {We can continue to attack! We are aiming for ...g4 next, with our knight also supporting that square.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: Other 1st Moves / Study 1: Basically Any Other White 1st Move / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/3c0940d2-f2b7-4f2c-869f-c40d913a5178/studies/6e5681e6-8b70-4ab5-a817-4ab378ad3369/lines"]

1.d4 {1.d4 is technically not a move 1 deviation, but there could be move 2 deviations. We will play 1... g6 as we normally do.} 1...g6 2.e3 {White could play in the form of some stonewall with an early f4. The idea is to gain space in the center. But because of our setup, we're well-equipped to face it! Nothing changes yet, as we continue our development.} 2...Bg7 3.Bd3 {White develops their bishop. But now we will play ...d6, with the idea to go ...e5.} 3...d6 4.f4 {Even if White plays f4! We don't want to allow them the central space here because it would be hard for us to develop. In this setting, fighting in the center immediately is the way.} 4...e5 5.fxe5 {White exchanges. We'll take back with the d-pawn, keeping a pawn in the center.} 5...dxe5 6.Nf3 {White continues to develop, as taking on e5 would allow us to activate our bishop. We'll develop as well and continue to support e5.} 6...Nc6 7.O-O {White castles.} 7...Nh6 {And we will activate our knight to h6 with a solid position. Our ideas after castling mainly consist of ...f5. If we get this additional space, dynamics change and we could consider pushing further.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: Other 1st Moves / Study 1: Basically Any Other White 1st Move / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/3c0940d2-f2b7-4f2c-869f-c40d913a5178/studies/6e5681e6-8b70-4ab5-a817-4ab378ad3369/lines"]

1.f4 {The Bird's opening might look a little bit ridiculous, but it is extremely viable, and has been played by strong players. It's simply an alternative way to control the e5-square. We'll play ...g6.} 1...g6 2.Nf3 {White develops normally behind the pawn, with more control over the center. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.g3 {This is a typical idea in the Bird's opening. White connects the pawns on the kingside and wants to activate their bishop behind the chain. Our idea is to go ...e5, though, and fight against White's structure!} 3...d6 4.Bg2 {White continues with their development. We now get our critical pawn break in the center.} 4...e5 5.fxe5 {White exchanges. We'll capture back with the pawn, maintaining control of the center} 5...dxe5 6.e4 {White now challenges the center with a pawn. We'll develop our knight to control more central squares. Once we've accomplished what we wanted in the center, normal development will ensue.} 6...Nc6 7.O-O {White castles, and could use the semi-open file to their advantage.} 7...Nge7 {We develop our knight to e7 with the future possibility of playing ...f5. This would give us further space in the center and on the kingside. We'll castle soon as well.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: Other 1st Moves / Study 1: Basically Any Other White 1st Move / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/3c0940d2-f2b7-4f2c-869f-c40d913a5178/studies/6e5681e6-8b70-4ab5-a817-4ab378ad3369/lines"]

1.b3 {White could try 1.b3, with the idea of Bb2. They want to give up the center temporarily and then fight for it later. This is a rare instance where we won't play ...g6 immediately, as it can get uncomfortable with White's bishop challenging the diagonal.} 1...e5 2.Bb2 {White follows through, attacking our pawn. We'll go ...Nc6 to defend it.} 2...Nc6 3.e3 {White goes e3, possibly with the idea to go f4 and unleash the bishop. This is the common idea in the Larsen; White plays against the e5-pawn. Now that the diagonal is clogged up, we can go ...g6 and continue our Modern setup.} 3...g6 4.Bb5 {Threatening to take the knight and to take on e5 is another option White can choose. That's why ...Bg7 is logical to defend the pawn.} 4...Bg7 5.f4 {Now our e-pawn is pinned, so f4 is a very interesting to try and exploit it. We have to be prepared for these tricky lines White can throw our way. The best course of action is supporting our knight which is defending the center.} 5...Nge7 6.Nf3 {White continues to pressure the center. We'll continue to protect it.} 6...d6 7.fxe5 {White may give in and capture on e5. Recapturing now would actually allow them to take again since the knight on c6 is pinned.} 7...O-O {We'll castle instead, as now their pawn is pinned, and we can recapture it on the next move! White hasn't achieved any advantage playing like this. If we know our lines, it's easy to equalize.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: Other 1st Moves / Study 1: Basically Any Other White 1st Move / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/3c0940d2-f2b7-4f2c-869f-c40d913a5178/studies/6e5681e6-8b70-4ab5-a817-4ab378ad3369/lines"]

1.b3 {White could try 1.b3, with the idea of Bb2. They want to give up the center temporarily and then fight for it later. This is a rare instance where we won't play ...g6 immediately, as it can get uncomfortable with White's bishop challenging the diagonal.} 1...e5 2.Bb2 {White follows through, attacking our pawn. We'll go ...Nc6 to defend it.} 2...Nc6 3.e3 {White goes e3, possibly with the idea to go f4 and unleash the bishop. This is the common idea in the Larsen; White plays against the e5-pawn. Now that the diagonal is clogged up, we can go ...g6 and continue our Modern setup.} 3...g6 4.f4 {Trying to exploit the pin. We'll fianchetto and protect the center.} 4...Bg7 5.Nf3 {White pressures the center further, which is to be expected.} 5...d6 {We'll protect the center further, which is to be expected too! Our position is stable and our center is safe. We'll finish kingside development next and castle.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: Other 1st Moves / Study 1: Basically Any Other White 1st Move / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/3c0940d2-f2b7-4f2c-869f-c40d913a5178/studies/6e5681e6-8b70-4ab5-a817-4ab378ad3369/lines"]

1.g3 {White could try with 1.g3, trying to fianchetto the light-squared bishop. The idea is to develop first and build for the center later. We'll still go ...g6 here in our Modern spirits.} 1...g6 2.Bg2 {White fianchettoes. We'll do the same. Now Nf3 and ...e5 transposes to the Reti.} 2...Bg7 3.c3 {So let's say White plays something else, like c3 to potentially build for d4 and restrict our bishop. We'll gladly take the center here, anticipating White's move.} 3...e5 4.Qb3 {Now White goes for Qb3. The queen is pressuring the b7-pawn together with the bishop. An interesting defense is ...Na6, threatening ...Nc5 if White captures.} 4...Na6 5.Nf3 {If they develop... We can harass them. This opens up our bishop again and forces their knight to move.} 5...e4 6.Nd4 {White jumps to the center. We've succesfully cut off White's bishop, but now we have to protect our central pawn.} 6...Nf6 7.O-O {White castles...} 7...O-O {And so do we. We have an amazing center and healthy piece development. We'll play ...d5 next and leave our bishop active against White's passive one. Then, we can develop flexibly.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: Other 1st Moves / Study 1: Basically Any Other White 1st Move / Variation 12"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/3c0940d2-f2b7-4f2c-869f-c40d913a5178/studies/6e5681e6-8b70-4ab5-a817-4ab378ad3369/lines"]

1.b3 {White could try 1.b3, with the idea of Bb2. They want to give up the center temporarily and then fight for it later. This is a rare instance where we won't play ...g6 immediately, as it can get uncomfortable with White's bishop challenging the diagonal.} 1...e5 2.Bb2 {White follows through, attacking our pawn. We'll go ...Nc6 to defend it.} 2...Nc6 3.e3 {White goes e3, possibly with the idea to go f4 and unleash the bishop. This is the common idea in the Larsen; White plays against the e5-pawn. Now that the diagonal is clogged up, we can go ...g6 and continue our Modern setup.} 3...g6 4.d4 {Confrontational, but it does not scare us. We'll just exchange.} 4...exd4 5.exd4 {White captures back.} 5...d5 {And we'll establish a pawn in the center. Our bishop is coming to g7 and White's will be the one staring at their own pawn. After that, normal development will follow.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: Other 1st Moves / Study 1: Basically Any Other White 1st Move / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/3c0940d2-f2b7-4f2c-869f-c40d913a5178/studies/6e5681e6-8b70-4ab5-a817-4ab378ad3369/lines"]

1.Nf3 1...g6 2.g3 {White employs what's called the "King's Indian Attack", preparing Bg2. It's like the KID, but on the White side! We'll fianchetto, naturally.} 2...Bg7 3.d4 {White may choose to take the center immediately. This is more Reti style. We'll continue to play in the style of the Modern. Our setup works against most of White's choices.} 3...d6 4.Bg2 {White develops to g2. We have a sneaky idea, to go ...Bd7 and ...Qc8, followed by ...Bh3 to exchange their fianchetto Bishop. We saw this idea in Chapter 10.} 4...Bd7 5.O-O {White castles. We'll follow through. There's not much White can do about it. And not many White players are prepared to trade their light-squared bishop early in the KIA.} 5...Qc8 6.e3 {White connects the pawns. There is no rush with ...Bh3 as it can hardly be stopped. We'll activate our knight first.} 6...Nc6 7.c4 {White controls the center further. We'll go ahead and play ...Bh3 now, forcing the exchange.} 7...Bh3 8.Bxh3 {White takes it. We'll take back and now our queen is active.} 8...Qxh3 9.Ng5 {Expect to see it attacked immediately, though. This is part of why White captured on h3 in the first place. We'll drop back to d7.} 9...Qd7 10.h4 {White attempts an attack of their own with h4, trying to play h5. But it won't last for long. We'll attack the knight.} 10...h6 11.Nf3 {White drops back.} 11...Qh3 {And we will bring the queen back to h3 as well, where it's the strongest! Of course, we can't attack without more pieces present. Which is why we will finish development quickly and castle.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: Other 1st Moves / Study 1: Basically Any Other White 1st Move / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/3c0940d2-f2b7-4f2c-869f-c40d913a5178/studies/6e5681e6-8b70-4ab5-a817-4ab378ad3369/lines"]

1.Nf3 1...g6 2.g3 {White employs what's called the "King's Indian Attack", preparing Bg2. It's like the KID, but on the White side! We'll fianchetto, naturally.} 2...Bg7 3.Bg2 {White continues their similar setup to ours. Honestly, if White goes this long without claiming the center, we should go ahead and do it ourselves.} 3...e5 4.O-O {White could also castle here and go for a different setup, aiming for d4. This gives Black a lot of space and isn't played often at the highest level, but we should learn how to face it anyway. Grabbing central space with the two pawns is always good.} 4...d5 5.d4 {White could try and challenge the center with d4... But ...e4 simply grabs more space and attacks the knight at the same time.} 5...e4 6.Ne1 {One of the most common moves is retreating the knight all the way to the e1-square. As we can see, this is already pretty passive for White! Their main idea, however, is to expand with c4 and challenge the center. Developing here is never wrong, preparing castling.} 6...Nf6 7.c4 {The most logical continuation. In this line, I recommend strengthening the center with ...c6. We'll castle, develop the light-squared bishop, and then activate the queenside knight either to d7 or c6 if White trades in the center.} 7...c6 8.Nc3 {White continues with standard development, putting more pressure to the center. Let's bring the king to safety.} 8...O-O 9.Bg5 {Don't be afraid of White pinning your knight. It is sufficiently defended and you can always kick out the bishop with ...h6! Activating the light-squared bishop with more defense in the center first.} 9...Be6 10.Nc2 {White could try to reactivate the knight to c2. Now we ask the bishop a question.} 10...h6 11.Bxf6 {If they take... We can simply recapture with the bishop.} 11...Bxf6 12.cxd5 {Let's show the plans for both sides if White decides to trade in the center as well.} 12...cxd5 {From this position forward, White might want to undermine our structure with moves like f3. Activating the knight to b4 and the queen to d2 are good developing moves. On the other hand, we've almost finished development. We'll play ...Nc6, activate the queen and connect the rooks.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: Other 1st Moves / Study 1: Basically Any Other White 1st Move / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/49d71d72-7d0e-4b55-bb66-5f257e37b4fc/chapters/3c0940d2-f2b7-4f2c-869f-c40d913a5178/studies/6e5681e6-8b70-4ab5-a817-4ab378ad3369/lines"]

1.c4 {The focus in the last chapter will be essentially all viable openings where White does not employ e4/d4, or d4/c4. While not the most traditional, these are useful lines to know. White goes for 1.c4. We'll respond with ...g6 here.} 1...g6 2.Nc3 {White develops with Nc3. We'll fianchetto.} 2...Bg7 3.g3 {White often goes g3, with the idea to go Bg2, and control the d5-square in conjunction with their other pieces. Since White hasn't immediately challenged the center, we'll take some space.} 3...e5 4.Bg2 {White develops the bishop to the long diagonal. We're going to establish a pawn chain followed by ...f5 to control the center even more.} 4...d6 5.d3 {White does something similar. And we continue with our plan.} 5...f5 6.e4 {White challenges our pawn center with e4. We don't really have to react to it, though, as our position is pretty solid.} 6...Nf6 7.Nge2 {White develops and prepares to castle. We will castle as well, and the rook could be useful on the semi-open file.} 7...O-O 8.h3 {White makes a waiting move. How should we continue here? Well, we should go for full central control, and ...c6 prepares the ...d5 advancement in this case! White has given us the freedom to do this.} 8...c6 9.Be3 {White develops logically, maybe keeping O-O-O as an option.} 9...Be6 {And we'll continue to develop towards the center. The move ...d5 is coming which will bring life to our pieces. After that, we can develop the knight to c6 if White takes on d5 or to another square, dependent on how the position develops. All in all, Black is doing well.} *